OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026", "title": "The Mahler Conjecture for General Convex Bodies", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "087", "family_title": "The Mahler conjectures, functional inequalities and polar-product symplectic width", "abstract_html": "We resolve the Mahler conjecture for general convex bodies positively. For every convex body \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), <i>n</i> ≥ 1, with Santaló point \\(s(K)\\), \\(|K|\\,|(K-s(K))^\\circ|\\ge (n+1)^{n+1}/(n!)^2\\), with equality exactly for simplices.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**General Mahler conjecture — paper:", "statement": "<p>**General Mahler conjecture — paper: Theorem 1.1.**</p><p>Let \\(n\\ge 1\\). A convex body \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) is a compact convex set with nonempty interior. For \\(z\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\), define\n\\[\n(K-z)^\\circ=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^n:\\langle y,x-z\\rangle\\le1\\text{ for every }x\\in K\\}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(|A|\\) for Lebesgue volume. The Santaló point \\(s(K)\\) is the unique minimizer of \\(z\\mapsto |(K-z)^\\circ|\\), and\n\\[\nP(K)=|K|\\,|(K-s(K))^\\circ|\n     =\\inf_{z\\in\\operatorname{int}K}|K|\\,|(K-z)^\\circ|.\n\\]</p><p>Then\n\\[\nP(K)\\ge \\frac{(n+1)^{n+1}}{(n!)^2}.\n\\]\nEquality holds if and only if \\(K\\) is a simplex.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(n=1\\), \\(K=[a,b]\\), and\n\\[\n|K|\\,|(K-z)^\\circ|\n=(b-a)\\left(\\frac1{z-a}+\\frac1{b-z}\\right)\\ge4,\n\\]\nwith equality at the midpoint. Every one-dimensional convex body is a simplex.</p><p>For \\(n\\ge2\\), Proposition 1 reduces the inequality to the cone bound (1).\nPropositions 2–4 provide the scalar input; Propositions 5–6 construct and normalize the projection field. Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\nm^{-1}\\log(\\chi_C(V)\\chi_D(U))\\ge-\\mathcal E,\n\\]\nand Propositions 8–10 prove \\(\\mathcal E\\le0\\).\nThus \\(\\chi_C(V)\\chi_D(U)\\ge1\\), giving the required lower bound at every interior translation point.</p><p>Suppose equality holds in the cone bound. Then\n\\(0\\ge-\\mathcal E\\ge0\\), so Proposition 10 gives diagonal support of \\(\\nu\\) and \\(\\beta=0\\).\nThe diagonal support implies\n\\[\n0=\\nu[(x-y)^2]\n=\\frac1m\\sum_\\ell\\mathbb E\\|[L,M_\\ell]\\|_{\\rm HS}^2\n=\\frac1m\\sum_{\\ell,k}\\|[M_k,M_\\ell]\\|_{\\rm HS}^2.\n\\]\nHence all \\(M_\\ell\\) commute. Choose a fixed orthonormal basis simultaneously diagonalizing them, so \\(L\\) is diagonal for every \\(G\\).</p><p>The vanishing of \\(\\beta\\) means\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\bigl((P_z)_{ii}-1_{\\{x_i\\le z\\}}\\bigr)(x_i-z)=0\n\\]\nfor almost every \\((G,z)\\). Every summand is nonnegative. Away from spectral endpoints, the diagonal entries of \\(P_z\\) therefore equal those of \\(\\Theta_z(L)\\). A positive semidefinite matrix with a zero diagonal entry has a zero row and column. Apply this to \\(P_z\\) at a zero entry and \\(I-P_z\\) at an entry equal to one, obtaining\n\\[\nP_z=\\Theta_z(L)\n\\]\nalmost everywhere.</p><p>Fubini gives one fixed layer at which \\(D\\Pi_C(Z+\\xi_z)\\) is diagonal almost surely. Since this Gaussian has an everywhere positive density, \\(D\\Pi_C\\) is diagonal Lebesgue-almost everywhere in the fixed basis.</p><p>The projection is globally Lipschitz. Its cross weak derivatives vanish, so\n\\[\n\\Pi_C(x_1,\\ldots,x_m)=(f_1(x_1),\\ldots,f_m(x_m)).\n\\]\nIndeed, mollification preserves vanishing cross derivatives, and local uniform convergence gives this assertion for the projection.</p><p>Its image is \\(C\\), and is the product of the ranges of \\(f_i\\).\nSince \\(\\Pi_C(0)=0\\), the \\(i\\)-th range is exactly\n\\[\n\\{t\\in\\mathbb R:te_i\\in C\\},\n\\]\na closed convex cone. Solidity excludes \\(\\{0\\}\\); pointedness excludes \\(\\mathbb R\\).\nEvery factor is a half-line. Thus the normalized cone is an orthant up to signs, and the original cone is generated by \\(m\\) independent rays. Proposition 1 then shows that \\(K\\) is a simplex. Apply this argument at the attained minimizing center when equality holds in \\(P(K)\\).</p><p>Conversely, affine invariance reduces a simplex to\n\\[\nK=\\operatorname{conv}\\{0,e_1,\\ldots,e_n\\},\\qquad\nz_0=\\frac1m\\mathbf1,\\qquad m=n+1.\n\\]\nThe polar inequalities give\n\\[\n(K-z_0)^\\circ\n=\\operatorname{conv}\\{m\\mathbf1,-me_1,\\ldots,-me_n\\}.\n\\]\nExplicitly, for \\(s_y=\\sum_i y_i\\), the inequalities are\n\\(s_y\\ge-m\\), \\(y_i\\le1+s_y/m\\); the barycentric coefficients\n\\[\n\\alpha_0=(s_y+m)/m^2,\\qquad\n\\alpha_i=\\alpha_0-y_i/m\n\\]\nare nonnegative exactly under these inequalities and sum to one.\nTherefore\n\\[\n|(K-z_0)^\\circ|\n=\\frac{m^n\\det(I+\\mathbf1\\mathbf1^T)}{n!}\n=\\frac{m^m}{n!},\n\\qquad |K|=\\frac1{n!}.\n\\]\nThis gives the claimed equality value.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Functional Mahler inequality — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Functional Mahler inequality — paper: Corollary 1.2.**</p><p>Let \\(n\\ge1\\), and let\n\\[\n\\varphi:\\mathbb R^n\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}\n\\]\nbe proper, lower-semicontinuous, and convex, with\n\\[\n0&lt;\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-\\varphi(x)}\\,dx&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n\\varphi^*(y)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}\\{\\langle x,y\\rangle-\\varphi(x)\\},\n\\qquad e^{-\\infty}=0.\n\\]</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-\\varphi(x)}\\,dx\\right)\n\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-\\varphi^*(y)}\\,dy\\right)\\ge e^n.\n\\]\nThe second integral may be infinite. No centering or normalization of \\(\\varphi\\) is required, and the constant \\(e^n\\) is sharp.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(f=e^{-\\varphi}\\). For \\(z\\in\\mathbb R^n\\), define\n\\[\nf^z(y)=\\inf_{\\{x:f(x)&gt;0\\}}\n       \\frac{e^{-\\langle x-z,y-z\\rangle}}{f(x)},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal P(f)=\n\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f\\right)\n\\inf_{z\\in\\mathbb R^n}\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f^z.\n\\]\nFradelizi and Meyer’s implication, cited in the paper as\n[8, Proposition 2(a)], says that the general geometric Mahler inequality in every dimension implies\n\\[\n\\mathcal P(f)\\ge e^n\n\\]\nfor every nonzero integrable log-concave \\(f\\).\nTheorem 1 supplies that all-dimensional hypothesis.\nAt \\(z=0\\),\n\\[\nf^0=e^{-\\varphi^*},\n\\]\nso the product in Theorem 2 is at least \\(\\mathcal P(f)\\).</p><p>The implication uses auxiliary convex bodies in dimensions \\(n+m\\) and passes to the limit \\(m\\to\\infty\\); no centering of \\(\\varphi\\) is imposed.</p><p>For sharpness, let \\(\\iota_A\\) be \\(0\\) on \\(A\\) and \\(+\\infty\\) outside. The potential\n\\[\n\\varphi(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i+\\iota_{[-1,\\infty)^n}(x)\n\\]\nhas transform\n\\[\n\\varphi^*(y)=n-\\sum_{i=1}^ny_i+\\iota_{(-\\infty,1]^n}(y).\n\\]\nTheir integrals are \\(e^n\\) and \\(1\\), respectively.\nThe geometric simplex equality classification does not itself classify functional equality, because the cited limiting implication does not transfer equality cases.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Entropy–transport inequality — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Entropy–transport inequality — paper: Corollary 8.1.**</p><p>Let \\(n\\ge1\\), and let\n\\[\n\\eta_i(dx)=e^{-V_i(x)}\\,dx,\\qquad i=1,2,\n\\]\nbe full-dimensional log-concave probability measures, where \\(V_i\\) are proper lower-semicontinuous convex functions. Assume essential continuity:\n\\[\ne^{-V_i}=0\n\\quad\\text{at }\\mathcal H^{n-1}\\text{-almost every point of }\\partial\\operatorname{supp}\\eta_i.\n\\]\nDefine the moment measures\n\\[\n\\nu_i=(\\nabla V_i)_\\#\\eta_i\n\\]\nand entropy relative to Lebesgue measure by\n\\[\nH(\\eta_i)=\\int\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{d\\eta_i}{dx}\\right)d\\eta_i\n         =-\\int V_i\\,d\\eta_i.\n\\]\nFor probability measures \\(\\mu_1,\\mu_2\\) with finite first moments, set\n\\[\n\\mathcal T(\\mu_1,\\mu_2)\n=\\inf_f\\left\\{\\int f\\,d\\mu_1+\\int f^*\\,d\\mu_2\\right\\},\n\\]\nwhere the infimum is over proper lower-semicontinuous convex functions\n\\(f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}\\). The cost may equal \\(+\\infty\\).</p><p>Then \\(\\nu_i\\) have finite first moments, \\(H(\\eta_i)\\) are finite, and\n\\[\nH(\\eta_1)+H(\\eta_2)\\le -3n+\\mathcal T(\\nu_1,\\nu_2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Gozlan’s equivalence between functional inverse Santaló inequalities and entropy–transport inequalities, cited in the paper as [9, Theorem 1.3], to Theorem 2 with \\(c=e\\).\nUnder the essential-continuity hypotheses, that equivalence supplies the stated moment and entropy finiteness and gives the additive constant\n\\[\n-n\\log(e\\,e^2)=-3n.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nH(\\eta_1)+H(\\eta_2)\\le-3n+\\mathcal T(\\nu_1,\\nu_2).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Center of polarity and cone reduction — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Center of polarity and cone reduction — paper: Section 2.1 and Lemma 2.1.**</p><p>Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be a convex body. The minimizing center defining \\(P(K)\\) exists, is unique, and is affine-equivariant; \\(P(K)\\) is invariant under invertible affine maps.</p><p>For \\(z_0\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\), put \\(m=n+1\\) and\n\\[\nC=\\{(y,t):t\\ge0,\\ y\\in t(K-z_0)\\},\\qquad\nD=C^*=\\{w:\\langle w,x\\rangle\\ge0\\text{ for all }x\\in C\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(V\\in\\operatorname{int}D\\), \\(U\\in\\operatorname{int}C\\), define\n\\[\n\\chi_C(V)=\\int_Ce^{-\\langle V,x\\rangle}\\,dx,\\qquad\n\\chi_D(U)=\\int_De^{-\\langle U,y\\rangle}\\,dy.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\chi_C((0,1))\\chi_D((0,m))\n=\\frac{(n!)^2}{m^m}|K|\\,|(K-z_0)^\\circ|.\n\\]</p><p>More generally, for any closed, convex, solid, pointed cone \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\), its positive dual \\(D=C^*\\), and interior \\(U,V\\), the Laplace integrals are finite. Their product and \\(\\langle U,V\\rangle\\) are preserved by\n\\[\n(C,D,U,V)\\mapsto(BC,B^{-T}D,BU,B^{-T}V)\n\\]\nfor every invertible linear map \\(B\\).</p><p>Consequently, the geometric inequality follows from\n\\[\n\\chi_C(V)\\chi_D(U)\\ge1\n\\quad\\text{whenever }\\langle U,V\\rangle=m. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nIf equality in this cone inequality forces \\(C\\) to be a linear image of an orthant, it forces the bounded section \\(K\\) to be a simplex.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 proves the center and affine-invariance assertions. Lemma 1.2 gives the cone identity and its invariances. Since \\(U=(0,m)\\), \\(V=(0,1)\\) satisfy \\(\\langle U,V\\rangle=m\\), inequality (1) gives the asserted geometric lower bound.</p><p>A cone with \\(m\\) independent generating rays has, in a bounded height-one section meeting every ray, the convex hull of the \\(m=n+1\\) ray intersections. Their affine independence follows from linear independence of the generators.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Existence, uniqueness, and affine behavior of the minimizing center — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Existence, uniqueness, and affine behavior of the minimizing center — paper: Section 2.1.**</p><p>For every convex body \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), the function\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto |(K-z)^\\circ|,\\qquad z\\in\\operatorname{int}K,\n\\]\nis strictly convex and tends to \\(+\\infty\\) as \\(z\\) approaches \\(\\partial K\\). It therefore has a unique interior minimizer.</p><p>For an invertible affine map \\(x\\mapsto Bx+a\\),\n\\[\n(BK+a-(Bz+a))^\\circ=B^{-T}(K-z)^\\circ.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(n\\ge2\\), polar coordinates give\n\\[\n|(K-z)^\\circ|\n=\\frac1n\\int_{S^{n-1}}(h_K(u)-\\langle z,u\\rangle)^{-n}\\,d\\sigma(u),\n\\]\nwhere \\(h_K(u)=\\sup_{x\\in K}\\langle u,x\\rangle\\). Strict convexity follows from strict convexity of \\(t\\mapsto t^{-n}\\) on \\((0,\\infty)\\): for two distinct centers, their inner products differ on a set of positive surface measure.</p><p>At a limiting boundary point choose a unit supporting normal \\(u_0\\). Then\n\\[\n\\varepsilon=h_K(u_0)-\\langle z,u_0\\rangle\\to0.\n\\]\nThe support functions \\(h_K(u)-\\langle z,u\\rangle\\), \\(z\\in K\\), have a common Lipschitz constant. On a spherical cap of radius \\(\\varepsilon\\) about \\(u_0\\), they are at most a constant times \\(\\varepsilon\\). The cap has measure at least a constant times \\(\\varepsilon^{n-1}\\), so its contribution is at least a constant times \\(\\varepsilon^{-1}\\).</p><p>For \\(n=1\\), use\n\\[\n(z-a)^{-1}+(b-z)^{-1},\\qquad K=[a,b].\n\\]\nThe displayed polar transformation follows directly from the defining inequalities. The two volume factors change by reciprocal determinants.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cone reduction — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Cone reduction — paper: Lemma 2.1.**</p><p>Under the cone definitions in Proposition 1,\n\\[\n\\chi_C((0,1))=n!|K|,\n\\qquad\n\\chi_D((0,m))=n!m^{-m}|(K-z_0)^\\circ|.\n\\]\nFor general closed convex solid pointed cone data, the finiteness and transformation assertions of Proposition 1 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>A point \\((w,t)\\) belongs to \\(D\\) exactly when\n\\[\nt\\ge\\sup_{x\\in K-z_0}\\langle-w,x\\rangle.\n\\]\nSince \\(0\\in\\operatorname{int}(K-z_0)\\), this forces \\(t\\ge0\\), and the slice at height \\(t\\) is\n\\[\n-t(K-z_0)^\\circ.\n\\]\nIntegration of the slices gives the two formulas.</p><p>For general cone data, interiority gives\n\\[\n\\langle V,x\\rangle\\ge c|x|\\quad(x\\in C)\n\\]\nand the analogous estimate for \\(U\\) on \\(D\\), proving finiteness. The linear transformations contribute determinant factors \\(|\\det B|\\) and \\(|\\det B|^{-1}\\), while preserving the exponential pairings.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Scalar profiles, identities, and real estimates — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Scalar profiles, identities, and real estimates — paper: Lemma 2.2, the first group of Proposition 2.3, and Lemma A.1.**</p><p>To distinguish the scalar profiles from the convex body and cone, denote the paper’s scalar \\(K,C\\) by \\(\\mathsf K,\\mathsf C\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\phi(x)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/2},\\qquad\np(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^x\\phi(y)\\,dy,\\qquad\n\\gamma f=\\int f(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx,\\qquad\nN=x\\partial_x-\\partial_x^2.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nX=\\frac p\\phi,\\quad Y=\\frac{1-p}{\\phi},\\quad\nf=-(1-p)-\\log p,\\quad j=-p-\\log(1-p),\n\\]\n\\[\nd=(1+xX)^2f+(1-xY)^2j,\\qquad\ng=\\frac12(1-X^2f-Y^2j),\\qquad v=\\log(XY).\n\\]\nFix the exact parameters\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nb=.602,\\quad c=.365,\\quad k=\\sqrt{b-c},\\quad r=.22,\\quad\nw=4.6,\\quad s=3.6,\\\\\n\\eta=.022,\\quad \\kappa=1.16,\\quad \\lambda=.65,\\quad a_R=7.5,\\\\\nt_-=-.022,\\quad t_+=.064,\\quad t_c=.021,\\quad t_r=.043,\\quad m_*=.352.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nEvery terminating decimal denotes an exact rational. Define\n\\[\n\\mathsf K=d-2g,\\qquad \\mathsf C=-d+(a_R-1)g'',\n\\]\n\\[\n\\pi(x)=r\\bigl(\\cos(w\\operatorname{arsinh}(x/s)),\n                  \\sin(w\\operatorname{arsinh}(x/s))\\bigr),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ell(x)=\\sqrt{b-r^2-d(x)},\\qquad q=k-\\ell,\\qquad\nu=(\\pi,q),\\qquad S_u=(2+N)u,\n\\]\n\\[\nF=d+|u|^2=c+2kq.\n\\]</p><p>The profiles satisfy\n\\[\n(N+2)g=d,\\qquad\nd'=2xd-v'-2g',\\qquad\nNd-v''=-(N+2)(d+g''). \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe radicand is uniformly positive; the profiles are smooth and all their derivatives have at most polynomial growth. The functions \\(d,g,v,\\mathsf K,\\mathsf C,q\\) are even.</p><p>Put \\(t=\\operatorname{arsinh}(x/s)\\), \\(a_t=s\\cosh t\\), and use dots for \\(t\\)-derivatives and\n\\[\nn_F=\\ddot F-\\tanh(t)\\dot F=a_t^2F''(x).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\nF&amp;\\inf F&amp;\\sup F&amp;|\\dot F|&amp;|\\ddot F|&amp;|n_F|\\\\ \\hline\n\\mathsf K&amp;-.007&amp;.0275&amp;.066&amp;.238&amp;\\text{--}\\\\\n\\mathsf C&amp;-.501&amp;-.341&amp;.247&amp;.79&amp;.79\\\\\nq&amp;.0775&amp;.2559&amp;.193&amp;.53&amp;.53\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|v'-x|\\le.319.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\lambda t_-^2+t_-\\mathsf C+\\mathsf K&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\lambda t_+^2+t_+\\mathsf C+\\mathsf K&lt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\n2(-.37)\\mathsf C-(.37)^2-4\\lambda\\mathsf K&gt;m_*^2.\n\\]\nThe joint derivative bound is\n\\[\nU_*=\n1.507\\max(0,-n_{\\mathsf K})\n+\\frac{12\\cdot.80}{m_*^2}\n\\left[1.25(\\dot{\\mathsf K}+t_c\\dot{\\mathsf C})^2\n       +5t_r^2\\dot{\\mathsf C}^{\\,2}\\right]\n\\le .54r^2w^2.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(|t|\\ge1.4\\),\n\\[\n-.0001\\le\\mathsf K\\le.0191,\\quad\n-.501\\le\\mathsf C\\le-.495,\\quad q\\ge.235,\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\dot{\\mathsf K}|\\le.0211,\\quad\n|\\dot{\\mathsf C}|\\le.0084,\\quad n_{\\mathsf K}\\ge0.\n\\]\nFor \\(|t|\\ge3.6\\),\n\\[\n0\\le\\mathsf K\\le.0008,\\quad\n|\\mathsf C+.5|\\le.0002,\\quad q\\ge.255.\n\\]\nFor \\(|x|\\ge5\\), \\(q\\ge.2203\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty(\\mathsf K)_+\\,dx\\le.422,\\qquad\n\\int_0^{s\\sinh(1.4)}(\\mathsf K)_+\\,dx\\le.126.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 proves the identities.</p><p>Apply Lemma 2.4 to the first thirteen rows of Lemma 2.2. In particular,\n\\[\nd\\le.50015,\\qquad b-r^2-d\\ge.05345&gt;0\n\\]\non \\(0\\le t\\le3.6\\), before any division by \\(\\ell\\).</p><p>For \\(l_0=1/(2\\ell)\\),\n\\[\n\\dot l_0=2l_0^3\\dot d,\\qquad\n\\ddot l_0=2l_0^3\\ddot d+12l_0^5\\dot d^2,\n\\]\ngiving \\(l_0\\le2.164\\), \\(|\\dot l_0|\\le2.8\\), \\(|\\ddot l_0|\\le19.2\\).\nThe identity for product chords,\n\\[\nEF-J_{EF}\n=E(F-J_F)+J_F(E-J_E)\n-\\theta(1-\\theta)(E_{i+1}-E_i)(F_{i+1}-F_i),\n\\]\ntherefore gives chord errors\n\\[\n.000042,\\ .0016,\\ .0095,\\ .012,\\ .003\n\\]\nfor \\(q,\\dot q,\\ddot q,n_q,S_q\\), respectively. The value errors of\n\\(\\mathsf K,\\mathsf C\\) sharpen to \\(.238/12800,.79/12800\\).</p><p>The expression \\(U_*\\) is convex in\n\\((-n_{\\mathsf K},\\dot{\\mathsf K},\\dot{\\mathsf C})\\).\nIts chord values are at most \\(.537\\). With\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_c=R(.00222),\\qquad\nE_*=R(.000673)+t_c\\varepsilon_c,\n\\]\nthe additional error is bounded by\n\\[\n1.507R(.00402)+\\frac{12\\cdot.80}{m_*^2}\n\\left[\n1.25E_*\\{2(.06542+t_c.2456)+E_*\\}\n+5t_r^2\\varepsilon_c(2\\cdot.2456+\\varepsilon_c)\n\\right]&lt;.010.\n\\]\nThus \\(U_*&lt;.547&lt;.54r^2w^2\\). The three covariance expressions have chord errors below\n\\(.000024,.000024,.0001\\), so their node margins persist.</p><p>For \\(t\\ge1.4\\), \\(n_{\\mathsf K,i}\\ge.00315&gt;R(.00402)\\); the restricted bounds follow. The condition \\(x\\ge5\\) is covered because \\(s\\sinh(45/40)&lt;5\\).</p><p>Convexity of positive part bounds \\((\\mathsf K)_+\\) by the chord through its endpoint positive parts, plus \\(.238/12800\\). Integration against \\(dx=s\\cosh t\\,dt\\) and summation by parts yields exactly the finite sums in Lemma 2.2. Lemma 2.5 supplies the overlapping infinite tail, giving the total integral bound \\(.335+.086&lt;.422\\), and all remaining tail assertions.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nY^{(j)}(x)=(-1)^j\\int_0^\\infty y^je^{-xy-y^2/2}\\,dy,\\qquad\n|Y^{(j)}(x)|\\le j!x^{-j-1}\\quad(x\\ge1).\n\\]\nTogether with \\(Y(x)\\asymp x^{-1}\\), the stable formulas, reflection, and the uniform positive radicand bound, this gives smoothness and polynomial derivative growth.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Profile identities — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Profile identities — paper: Lemma 2.2.**</p><p>For the scalar profiles in Proposition 2, all three identities in (2) hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>One has\n\\[\nX'=1+xX,\\quad Y'=xY-1,\\quad\nf'=-\\phi(1-p)/p,\\quad j'=\\phi p/(1-p).\n\\]\nDirect substitution gives\n\\[\nX^2f'+Y^2j'=0,\\quad\nXX'f'+YY'j'=-1,\\quad\n(X')^2f'+(Y')^2j'=-v'.\n\\]\nAlso \\(X''=xX'+X\\), \\(Y''=xY'+Y\\). Thus\n\\[\ng'=-(XX'f+YY'j),\n\\]\nand one more differentiation proves \\((N+2)g=d\\). Differentiating\n\\(d=(X')^2f+(Y')^2j\\) proves the first-order identity for \\(d\\).\nDifferentiating the first identity twice yields\n\\[\nd''=(N+4)g'';\n\\]\ncombining this with the derivative of the second identity proves the third.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite certificates — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Finite certificates — paper: Lemma A.3.**</p><p>Use the notation of Proposition 2, and evaluate at\n\\[\nt_i=i/40,\\qquad 0\\le i\\le151.\n\\]\nThe following are exact rational enclosures. The second-difference bounds apply to \\(0\\le i\\le150\\), with parity supplying index \\(-1\\); the first-difference bounds have the same range.\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\nF&amp;F_i&amp;|\\Delta^2F_i|&amp;|\\Delta F_i|\\\\ \\hline\nd&amp;[.3862,.5]&amp;.000260&amp;--\\\\\n\\dot d&amp;\\pm.1338&amp;.00092&amp;.01037\\\\\n\\ddot d&amp;\\pm.415&amp;.00421&amp;.03670\\\\\n\\mathsf K&amp;[-.00692,.02737]&amp;.000148&amp;--\\\\\n\\dot{\\mathsf K}&amp;\\pm.06542&amp;.000673&amp;--\\\\\n\\ddot{\\mathsf K}&amp;\\pm.2355&amp;.00373&amp;--\\\\\nn_{\\mathsf K}&amp;\\pm.2355&amp;.00402&amp;--\\\\\n\\mathsf C&amp;[-.5,-.3413]&amp;.00049&amp;--\\\\\n\\dot{\\mathsf C}&amp;\\pm.2456&amp;.00222&amp;--\\\\\n\\ddot{\\mathsf C}&amp;\\pm.780&amp;.0129&amp;--\\\\\nn_{\\mathsf C}&amp;\\pm.780&amp;.0138&amp;--\\\\\nR_v=v'-x&amp;\\pm.318&amp;.00167&amp;--\\\\\nV_2=v''&amp;\\pm1.06&amp;.00186&amp;--\\\\\nq&amp;[.0777,.25525]&amp;--&amp;--\\\\\n\\dot q&amp;\\pm.1908&amp;--&amp;.0127\\\\\n\\ddot q&amp;\\pm.508&amp;--&amp;.047\\\\\nn_q&amp;\\pm.508&amp;--&amp;--\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nAt all these nodes,\n\\[\nU_*\\le.537,\\quad\n\\lambda t_-^2+t_-\\mathsf C+\\mathsf K\\ge.00090,\\quad\n\\lambda t_+^2+t_+\\mathsf C+\\mathsf K\\le-.00074,\n\\]\n\\[\n2(-.37)\\mathsf C-(.37)^2-4\\lambda\\mathsf K\\ge.132.\n\\]\nFor \\(56\\le i\\le144\\),\n\\[\n0\\le\\mathsf K_i\\le.019,\\quad\n|\\dot{\\mathsf K}_i|\\le.0207,\\quad n_{\\mathsf K,i}\\ge.00315,\n\\]\n\\[\n-.5\\le\\mathsf C_i\\le-.49512,\\quad\n|\\dot{\\mathsf C}_i|\\le.0071,\\quad q_i\\ge.2353.\n\\]\nFor \\(45\\le i\\le144\\), \\(q_i\\ge.2204\\).</p><p>For \\(i=56,144\\), respectively,\n\\[\nx_i\\bigl((\\mathsf K_i)_++ .238/12800\\bigr)\n-40\\sum_{j=1}^i(a_{t_j}-a_{t_{j-1}})\n \\bigl((\\mathsf K_j)_+-(\\mathsf K_{j-1})_+\\bigr)\n\\le .126,\\ .335.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By reflection it suffices to calculate for \\(x\\ge0\\). Write\n\\[\nu_0=1-p=Y\\phi,\\qquad\nh(u_0)=\\frac{-\\log(1-u_0)-u_0}{u_0^2},\\qquad h(0)=\\frac12,\n\\]\nand \\(B_0=1-xY\\). The stable formulas used for the certificates are\n\\[\nY=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-xy-y^2/2}\\,dy,\\qquad\nj=x^2/2+\\log\\sqrt{2\\pi}-\\log Y-p,\n\\]\n\\[\nd=B_0^2j+(1-pB_0)^2h,\\qquad\n\\mathsf K=d+Y^2(j+p^2h)-1,\n\\]\n\\[\ng'=Y(B_0j-(1-pB_0)ph),\\qquad g''=xg'-\\mathsf K,\n\\]\n\\[\nR_v=\\phi/p-B_0/Y,\\qquad\nV_2=2-(\\phi/p)(x+\\phi/p)-B_0/Y^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nD_1=x(2d-1)-R_v-2g',\\qquad\nD_2=2d+2xD_1-2g''-V_2,\n\\]\n\\[\nG_3=xg''+3g'-D_1,\\qquad\nG_4=xG_3+4g''-D_2.\n\\]\nFor \\(F=d,\\mathsf K,\\mathsf C\\), respectively, use\n\\[\n(P_F,Q_F)\n=(D_1,D_2),\\quad(D_1-2g',D_2-2g''),\\quad\n(-D_1+6.5G_3,-D_2+6.5G_4),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\dot F=a_tP_F,\\qquad \\ddot F=a_t^2Q_F+xP_F,\\qquad n_F=a_t^2Q_F.\n\\]\nAfter verifying \\(d_i\\le.5\\), evaluate\n\\[\n\\dot q=\\frac{\\dot d}{2\\ell},\\qquad\n\\ddot q=\\frac{\\ddot d/2+\\dot q^2}{\\ell},\\qquad\nn_q=\\frac{n_d/2+\\dot q^2}{\\ell},\n\\]\n\\[\nS_q=2q+\\frac{x}{a_t}(1+a_t^{-2})\\dot q-\\ddot q/a_t^2.\n\\]</p><p>The primitive enclosures are as follows.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Put\n\\[\nE_i=\\left(\\sum_{m=0}^{26}\\frac{(i/160)^m}{m!}\n                 +[-10^{-27},10^{-27}]\\right)^4.\n\\]\nThis encloses \\(e^{i/40}\\), so\n\\[\ns(E_i-E_i^{-1})/2,\\qquad s(E_i+E_i^{-1})/2\n\\]\nenclose \\(x_i,a_{t_i}\\); intersect the first with \\([0,90]\\).\nFor \\(i\\le73\\), enclose \\(\\phi(x_i)\\) using the same exponential sum with argument\n\\(-x_i^2/128\\), raised to the \\(64\\)th power and divided by \\(\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\).\nFor \\(i\\ge74\\), use \\([0,10^{-26}]\\). Every Taylor argument has modulus at most \\(1\\), and \\(3/27!&lt;10^{-27}\\) bounds the exponential remainder.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For \\(i\\le30\\), put\n\\[\ny_m=x_i(-x_i^2/2)^m/m!,\\qquad\nL_i=\\sum_{m=0}^{39}\\frac{y_m}{2m+1}+[0,y_{40}/81],\n\\]\nand use\n\\[\nY=(1/2-L_i/\\sqrt{2\\pi})/\\phi.\n\\]\nFor \\(i\\ge31\\), start \\(T_{34}=[0,34/x_i]\\), recursively set\n\\[\nT_m=m/(x_i+T_{m+1}),\\qquad m=33,\\ldots,1,\n\\]\nand take \\(Y=1/(x_i+T_1)\\). Indeed, with\n\\[\nI_m(x)=\\int_0^\\infty y^me^{-xy-y^2/2}\\,dy,\n\\]\nintegration by parts gives\n\\(mI_{m-1}=xI_m+I_{m+1}\\), and \\(xI_0+I_1=1\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Form \\(u_0=(Y\\phi)\\cap[0,1/2]\\), \\(p=1-u_0\\), and enclose \\(h\\) by\n\\[\n\\sum_{m=0}^{44}\\frac{u_0^m}{m+2}+[0,2u_0^{45}/47].\n\\]\nFor logarithms, scale the argument by a power of \\(2\\) into \\([1,2.01]\\), put\n\\(y=(z-1)/(z+1)\\), and use\n\\[\n2\\sum_{m=0}^{24}\\frac{y^{2m+1}}{2m+1},\n\\qquad\n\\text{error }\\le\\frac{2|y|^{51}}{51(1-y^2)}.\n\\]\nUse\n\\[\n3.14159265358979323846264338\n\\le\\pi\\le\n3.14159265358979323846264339.\n\\]\nThe endpoints follow from the arctangent series in\n\\(\\pi=16\\arctan(1/5)-4\\arctan(1/239)\\).\nFor square roots, consecutive multiples of \\(10^{-26}\\) enclosing each endpoint suffice.</p><p>All operations use their full interval ranges and outward rounding. Taking hulls and differences gives the displayed tables; direct substitution gives the simultaneous bounds and finite sums. In particular, the sums lie in\n\\[\n[.1250504493,.1250504494],\\qquad\n[.3344848649,.3344848650].\n\\]\nThe paper’s supplementary programs implement these rules with integer endpoints on a \\(10^{-70}\\) grid; the formulas and remainder bounds specify the certificates independently of the programs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Common analytic strip — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Common analytic strip — paper: Lemma A.4.**</p><p>As functions of \\(t\\), the profiles\n\\[\nd,\\mathsf K,\\mathsf C,R_v,V_2\n\\]\nare analytic in a neighborhood of \\(\\{|\\operatorname{Im}t|\\le.45\\}\\). The first three have modulus at most \\(1300\\), and the last two at most \\(11000\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\operatorname{Re}t\\ge0\\), write \\(x=A+iy=s\\sinh t\\). Then \\(A\\ge0\\) and\n\\[\ny^2\\le2.5+.25A^2.\n\\]\nThe Laplace integrals give\n\\[\n|Y|\\le\\min(1.254,1/A),\\qquad |B_0|\\le\\min(1,1/A^2),\n\\]\nomitting reciprocal bounds at \\(A=0\\), and\n\\[\n|1-p|\\le e^{y^2/2}(1-p(A))\\le1.75.\n\\]\nHere \\((1-p(A))e^{A^2/8}\\) decreases, using \\(A(1-p(A))\\le\\phi(A)\\).</p><p>If \\(A\\le.9\\), horizontal integration from \\(iy\\) gives \\(\\operatorname{Re}p\\ge1/2\\), since \\(A|y|&lt;\\pi/2\\). If \\(A\\ge.9\\),\n\\[\n|1-p|\\le(1-p(.9))e^{1.25+.125(.9)^2}&lt;.80.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\operatorname{Re}p&gt;0\\). The integral\n\\[\nh(u_0)=\\int_0^1\\frac{v}{1-u_0v}\\,dv\n\\]\nproves analyticity and \\(|h|\\le2.5\\).</p><p>If \\(T_A\\) is Cauchy with scale \\(A\\), its characteristic function gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}Y(A+iy)=\\sqrt{\\pi/2}\\,\n \\mathbb E e^{-(y+T_A)^2/2}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor \\(A\\le2\\), this is at least \\(\\sqrt{\\pi/2}e^{-2}/4&gt;.02\\).\nFor \\(A\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{Y(A+iy)}{Y(A)}-1\\right|\n\\le |y|/A\\le\\sqrt{7/8}&lt;.936,\n\\qquad\nY(A)&gt;.324/A.\n\\]\nHence \\(|Y|\\ge.02/(1+A)\\).</p><p>The principal logarithms of \\(p,Y\\) are analytic. Define\n\\[\n\\log(1-p)=\\log Y-x^2/2-\\log\\sqrt{2\\pi}.\n\\]\nThe preceding bounds imply\n\\[\n|j|\\le.625A^2+\\log(1+A)+10.5,\\quad |B_0j|\\le12,\\quad |Y^2j|\\le19.\n\\]\nThe stable formulas yield\n\\[\n|d|&lt;48,\\quad |\\mathsf K|&lt;99,\\quad |xg'|&lt;76,\\quad\n|\\mathsf C|&lt;1300.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n|\\phi/p|\\le7e^{-.375A^2},\\quad |B_0/Y|\\le100,\\quad\n|B_0/Y^2|\\le10000,\n\\]\nso \\(|R_v|\\le107\\) and \\(|V_2|&lt;11000\\).</p><p>Reflection gives the left-half bounds. On the intervening imaginary segment, \\(p\\) and \\(1-p\\) have real part \\(1/2\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Re}Y&gt;0\\). The formulas and their parity reflections agree near zero and hence analytically across that segment.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Interpolation from second differences — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Interpolation from second differences — paper: Lemma A.5.**</p><p>Put \\(R(M)=.536M+.00001\\). For any of\n\\[\nF,\\dot F,\\ddot F,n_F\\quad(F=d,\\mathsf K,\\mathsf C),\\qquad R_v,V_2,\n\\]\nthe error from the affine chord on a mesh cell in \\([0,3.6]\\) is at most \\(R(M)\\), where \\(M\\) bounds the absolute second differences on the sixteen-node stencil for that cell.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the cell \\([t_i,t_{i+1}]\\), use nodes \\(i-7,\\ldots,i+8\\), reflected by parity as necessary, and let \\(I_8\\) be their degree-\\(15\\) interpolant. Lemma 2.3 and Cauchy estimates across the strip gap \\(.09\\) bound the required functions on the inner strip by \\(4\\cdot10^5\\). The real interpolation remainder is at most\n\\[\n4\\cdot10^5\\prod_{j=0}^7\n \\left(\\frac{j+1/2}{40\\cdot.36}\\right)^2\n&lt;.00001.\n\\]</p><p>Normalize \\(t=t_i+\\theta/40\\), \\(0\\le\\theta\\le1\\). Let \\(I_l\\) interpolate nodes \\(1-l,\\ldots,l\\), so \\(I_1\\) is the central chord. If \\(J_l^\\pm\\) omit the leftmost or rightmost node,\n\\[\nI_l=\\frac{l-\\theta}{2l-1}J_l^-\n    +\\frac{\\theta+l-1}{2l-1}J_l^+.\n\\]\nThe weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Newton interpolation gives\n\\[\nJ_l^--I_{l-1}\n=\\frac{\\Delta^{2l-2}\\Psi_{1-l}}{(2l-2)!}\n \\prod_{k=2-l}^{l-1}(\\theta-k),\n\\]\nand the corresponding formula with \\(\\Psi_{2-l}\\) for \\(J_l^+\\).\nSince\n\\[\n|\\Delta^{2l-2}\\Psi_k|\\le2^{2l-4}M,\\qquad\n\\left|\\prod_{k=2-l}^{l-1}(\\theta-k)\\right|\n\\le\\prod_{j=0}^{l-2}(j+1/2)^2,\n\\]\nsumming \\(l=2,\\ldots,8\\) yields\n\\[\n|I_8-I_1|\n\\le M\\sum_{l=2}^8\\frac{2^{2l-4}}{(2l-2)!}\n             \\prod_{j=0}^{l-2}(j+1/2)^2\n=\\frac{4387}{8192}M&lt;.536M.\n\\]\nAdd the analytic remainder. The largest required index is \\(151\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tail derivative bounds — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Tail derivative bounds — paper: Lemma A.6.**</p><p>For \\(x\\ge64\\), put\n\\[\nD=x\\partial_x,\\qquad \\xi=x^{-2},\\qquad\nL_0=\\log x+\\frac12\\log(2\\pi)-\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe entries below bound \\(|D^jF|/\\xi\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\nF&amp;j=0&amp;j=1&amp;j=2\\\\ \\hline\n\\mathsf K-\\xi(L_0-\\frac32)&amp;.0064&amp;.027&amp;.11\\\\\nd-\\frac12&amp;.51&amp;1.02&amp;2.1\\\\\n\\mathsf C+\\frac12&amp;.55&amp;1.16&amp;2.61\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nOn this tail,\n\\[\nq\\in[.255,.2556],\\quad S_q\\in[.509,.512],\\quad\nV_2\\in[.999,1.001].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\xi_0=1/4096\\), \\(m_0=xY=1-\\xi n_0\\), where\n\\[\nn_0=\\int_0^\\infty ye^{-y-\\xi y^2/2}\\,dy,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nZ_0=\\log x+\\tfrac12\\log(2\\pi)-\\log m_0-p+p^2h.\n\\]\nThe exact expressions are\n\\[\nd=h+\\xi(\\tfrac12n_0^2-2phn_0)+\\xi^2n_0^2Z_0,\n\\]\n\\[\ng=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac14(1-m_0^2)-\\tfrac12\\xi m_0^2Z_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathsf K=(h-\\tfrac12)+\n \\xi\\{A_0+(m_0^2+\\xi n_0^2)Z_0\\},\n\\quad\nA_0=\\tfrac12n_0^2-2phn_0-n_0+\\tfrac12\\xi n_0^2.\n\\]</p><p>For numerical vectors indexed by \\(0,\\ldots,4\\), write \\(F\\preceq aV\\) when\n\\(|D^jF|\\le aV_j\\). Products are controlled by binomial convolution.\nLet \\(P_j=2^j\\), \\(I_j=1_{\\{j=0\\}}\\), \\(J_j=1_{\\{j=1\\}}\\).\nDifferentiation of the integral gives\n\\[\nn_0-1\\preceq3.1\\xi P.\n\\]\nThus, with\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=I+3.1\\xi_0P,\\qquad\n\\mathcal M=I+1.02\\xi_0P,\n\\]\none has \\(n_0\\preceq\\mathcal N\\), \\(m_0\\preceq\\mathcal M\\), and\n\\[\nm_0-1\\preceq\\xi P\\mathcal N,\\qquad P\\mathcal N\\le1.02P.\n\\]\nThe logarithmic series gives \\(-\\log m_0\\preceq1.04\\xi P\\). The Gaussian corrections satisfy\n\\[\nh-\\tfrac12,\\ ph-\\tfrac12,\\ p-1\\preceq10^{-6}\\xi^2P.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nZ_0-L_0\\preceq1.05\\xi P,\\qquad\nZ_0\\preceq(\\xi_0/\\xi)\\mathcal H,\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=4.59I+J+1.05\\xi_0P.\n\\]</p><p>Expansion of the algebraic corrections gives\n\\[\nA_0+\\tfrac32\\preceq10\\xi P,\\qquad\nm_0^2+\\xi n_0^2-1\\preceq3.2\\xi P.\n\\]\nSubtracting the main term of \\(\\mathsf K\\), the derivative vector in units of \\(\\xi\\) is bounded by\n\\[\n\\xi_0\\{10^{-6}P+P(11.05P+3.2P\\mathcal H)\\},\n\\]\nwhose first three entries are at most \\((.0064,.027,.11)\\).</p><p>Similarly,\n\\[\nd-\\tfrac12\\preceq\\xi E_d,\\qquad\nE_d=\\tfrac12P+\\xi_0(10^{-6}P+10P^2+P^2\\mathcal N^2\\mathcal H),\n\\]\nwhose first three entries are at most \\((.51,1.02,2.1)\\).</p><p>Writing\n\\[\ng-\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12\\xi n_0-\\tfrac14\\xi^2n_0^2-\\tfrac12\\xi m_0^2Z_0,\n\\]\nput\n\\[\nE_g=P(\\tfrac12\\mathcal N+.25\\xi_0P\\mathcal N^2+.5\\mathcal M^2\\mathcal H).\n\\]\nThen \\(g-\\tfrac14\\preceq\\xi_0E_g\\). Since \\(g''=\\xi(D^2-D)g\\), the derivative bounds for\n\\(\\mathsf C+\\tfrac12=-(d-\\tfrac12)+6.5g''\\) are controlled by\n\\[\nE_d+6.5\\xi_0P\\bigl((E_g)_{j+1}+(E_g)_{j+2}\\bigr)_{j=0}^2,\n\\]\ngiving \\((.55,1.16,2.61)\\).</p><p>Use\n\\[\n\\dot F=(a_t/x)DF,\\quad\nn_F=(a_t/x)^2(D^2-D)F,\\quad\n\\ddot F=n_F+DF,\\quad a_t/x\\le1.002.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n(D^2-D)\\mathsf K\n\\ge\\xi\\{6(L_0-1.5)-5-.11-.027\\}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe value estimates imply \\(0\\le\\mathsf K\\le.0008\\),\n\\(|\\mathsf C+.5|\\le.0002\\), and the stated derivative bounds.</p><p>The radicand obeys\n\\[\n\\ell^2\\ge.0536-.51\\xi_0&gt;.231^2,\n\\]\nso the formulas for \\(q\\) give\n\\[\nq\\in[.255,.2556],\\quad |\\dot q|&lt;.00055,\\quad |n_q|&lt;.0017,\n\\]\nand hence the stated \\(S_q\\) range.\nFinally,\n\\[\nV_2=2-(\\phi/p)(x+\\phi/p)-n_0/m_0^2,\\qquad\nR_v=\\phi/p-n_0/(xm_0),\n\\]\nwith \\(1-3\\xi\\le n_0\\le1\\), \\(1-\\xi\\le m_0\\le1\\), gives the \\(V_2\\) and \\(R_v\\) bounds.</p><p>These estimates also verify the covariance inequalities and \\(U_*&lt;.001\\) on the tail. Since\n\\(\\mathsf K\\le(\\log x)x^{-2}\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{x_{144}}^\\infty(\\mathsf K)_+\\,dx\n\\le\\frac{\\log64+1}{64}&lt;.086.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Pointwise layer inequalities — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Pointwise layer inequalities — paper: the second group of Proposition 2.3 and Lemma A.2.**</p><p>Use the scalar profiles and exact constants of Proposition 2. Define\n\\[\nb_m=\\frac{(1+t_+)(b-3\\eta)}{3(3(b+\\eta)-4c)},\\qquad\ne_0=.256,\\quad\\alpha=2.26,\\quad h_s=.5,\n\\]\n\\[\nH(z,x)=4c+4kq(x)+2(k-q(x))S_q(z)-2S_\\pi(z)\\cdot\\pi(x),\n\\]\n\\[\nH_0(z,x)=H(z,x)+v''(x)-2\\kappa.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n.113\\le S_q\\le.514,\\qquad |S_\\pi|\\le1.104,\n\\]\nand, for all \\(z,x\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH+t_cH_0-\\frac{t_r}{2}(h_s+H_0^2/h_s)\n&gt;&amp;\\,2\\bigl(b+\\eta+(b+\\eta-\\kappa)t_-\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;+2b_mS_q(z)S_q(x)+2e_0(2t_r)^2+H_0^2/\\alpha,\n\\end{aligned} \\qquad(3a)\n\\]\n\\[\n4c+2k(S_q(z)+S_q(x))-S_q(z)S_q(x)-S_\\pi(z)\\cdot S_\\pi(x)&gt;.25. \\tag{3b}\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\nb+\\eta+\\kappa t_-+.125&gt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1.053\\kappa^2}{8(b+\\eta+\\kappa t_-+.125)}&lt;e_0,\n\\qquad\ne_0+\\frac{\\alpha(\\log2+.25)}4&lt;.80.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The interpolation errors established in Proposition 2 are\n\\(.000042,.003,.00101\\) for \\(q,S_q,V_2\\).\nThe following table encloses the profiles on the indicated intervals of \\(|t|\\), in units \\(1/40\\); every entry is multiplied by \\(1000\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrr}\n&amp; q_{\\min}&amp;q_{\\max}&amp;S_{q,\\min}&amp;S_{q,\\max}&amp;V_{2,\\min}&amp;V_{2,\\max}&amp;|S_\\pi|_{\\max}\\\\ \\hline\n0:4&amp;77&amp;81&amp;113&amp;132&amp;725&amp;743&amp;804\\\\\n4:8&amp;80&amp;88&amp;125&amp;166&amp;740&amp;785&amp;814\\\\\n8:12&amp;87&amp;100&amp;159&amp;220&amp;782&amp;848&amp;831\\\\\n12:16&amp;99&amp;116&amp;213&amp;288&amp;845&amp;920&amp;853\\\\\n16:20&amp;115&amp;134&amp;281&amp;359&amp;917&amp;986&amp;877\\\\\n20:28&amp;133&amp;171&amp;352&amp;464&amp;983&amp;1055&amp;927\\\\\n28:40&amp;170&amp;211&amp;457&amp;504&amp;1041&amp;1059&amp;992\\\\\n40:68&amp;210&amp;245&amp;497&amp;513&amp;1009&amp;1044&amp;1072\\\\\n68+&amp;244&amp;256&amp;506&amp;514&amp;999&amp;1012&amp;1104\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe last row includes the tail bounds. For \\(\\rho=|S_\\pi|\\),\n\\[\n\\rho=r\\sqrt{(2+w^2/a_t^2)^2+(1+a_t^{-2})^2w^2x^2/a_t^2}.\n\\]\nWriting \\(y=\\tanh^2t\\), \\(v_1=(1-y)/s^2\\), differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dy}\\frac{\\rho^2}{r^2w^2}\n=(1+v_1)^2-\\frac{2(2+w^2v_1)}{s^2}\n-\\frac{2y(1+v_1)}{s^2}&gt;.27.\n\\]\nThus endpoint bounds suffice for \\(\\rho\\).</p><p>For rows \\(i_x,i_z\\), the scalar part of \\(H\\) increases in \\(q(x)\\) and \\(S_q(z)\\), since \\(q&lt;k\\) and \\(S_q&lt;2k\\). Therefore\n\\[\nH_\\pm=4c+4kq_{i_x}^\\pm+\n      2(k-q_{i_x}^\\pm)S_{i_z}^\\pm\\pm2r\\rho_{i_z}^+\n\\]\nenclose \\(H\\). Subtract the right side of (3a), replace \\(S_q(x)S_q(z)\\) by its row upper bound, and put \\(V=V_2(x)\\). The resulting function\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(H,V)={}&amp;H+t_c(H+V-2\\kappa)\n-\\left(\\frac{t_r}{2h_s}+\\frac1\\alpha\\right)(H+V-2\\kappa)^2\\\\\n&amp;-2(b+\\eta+(b+\\eta-\\kappa)t_-)\n-2b_mS_{i_z}^+S_{i_x}^+\n-2e_0(2t_r)^2-\\frac{t_rh_s}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nis concave. Its minimum over the rectangle is therefore at a vertex.\nThe \\(81\\) row pairs, with four vertices each, give lower bounds, multiplied by \\(1000\\) and minimized over \\(i_x\\),\n\\[\n11,\\ 14,\\ 33,\\ 64,\\ 63,\\ 39,\\ 54,\\ 45,\\ 40.\n\\]\nThis proves (3a).</p><p>The left side of (3b) is bounded below by\n\\[\n4c+4k(.113)-(.113)^2-(1.104)^2&gt;.25.\n\\]\nThe remaining inequalities follow by rational arithmetic and the logarithm enclosure already used in the scalar certificates.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Strict segment inequality — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Strict segment inequality — paper: the third group of Proposition 2.3 and Proposition B.1.**</p><p>Use the scalar profiles and constants of Proposition 2. For \\(x_-&lt;x_+\\), a bar denotes the uniform average in the physical coordinate \\(x\\) on \\([x_-,x_+]\\), and a subscript \\(\\sigma\\in\\{-,+\\}\\) denotes endpoint evaluation. Define\n\\[\ne_{\\mathsf K}=\\overline{\\mathsf K}-(\\mathsf K_++\\mathsf K_-)/2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Gamma=\\frac{.80}{m_*^2}\n \\left[1.25(\\mathsf K_+-\\mathsf K_-+\n                    t_c(\\mathsf C_+-\\mathsf C_-))^2\n       +5t_r^2(\\mathsf C_+-\\mathsf C_-)^2\\right].\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ne_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma\n&amp;+\\frac1{8\\lambda}\\sum_{\\sigma}\n \\left(\\overline{\\mathsf C}-\\mathsf C_{-\\sigma}\n                      -\\overline{|u-u_\\sigma|^2}\\right)^2\\\\\n&amp;+\\frac{t_+}{2}\\sum_\\sigma|\\bar u-u_\\sigma|^2\n&lt;\\overline{|u|^2}-|\\bar u|^2. \\qquad(4)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor coincident endpoints the two sides are equal.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write the endpoint \\(t\\)-coordinates as \\(l=m-h\\), \\(y=m+h\\). Reflection permits \\(m\\ge0\\), so \\(y\\ge|l|\\). The averaging density is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cosh t}{2\\cosh m\\sinh h}.\n\\]\nThe mean circle coordinate is\n\\[\nre^{iwm}(A_h+iB_h\\tanh m),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nA_h=\\frac{\\cos(wh)+w\\coth h\\sin(wh)}{1+w^2},\\qquad\nB_h=\\frac{\\coth h\\sin(wh)-w\\cos(wh)}{1+w^2}.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nf_*=\\frac{r^2}{1+w^2}\\left(w^2-\\frac{\\sin^2(wh)}{\\sinh^2h}\\right)&gt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\np_a=2r^2(1-A_h\\cos(wh)),\\qquad\np_d=-2r^2B_h\\tanh m\\sin(wh),\n\\]\n\\[\nb_\\sigma=\\overline{(q-q_\\sigma)^2},\\quad\nb_a=(b_++b_-)/2,\\quad b_d=(b_+-b_-)/2,\n\\]\n\\[\nc_a=\\overline{\\mathsf C}-(\\mathsf C_++\\mathsf C_-)/2,\\qquad\nc_d=(\\mathsf C_+-\\mathsf C_-)/2.\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\nA_h^2+B_h^2=\\frac{1+\\sin^2(wh)/\\sinh^2h}{1+w^2}\n\\]\nshows that the variance of the circle coordinates is at least \\(f_*\\).\nAlso\n\\[\n\\overline{|\\pi-\\pi_\\pm|^2}=p_a\\pm p_d,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\overline{\\mathsf C}-\\mathsf C_\\mp-\\overline{|u-u_\\pm|^2}\n=(c_a-p_a-b_a)\\pm(c_d-p_d-b_d).\n\\]\nWriting \\(V_u=\\overline{|u|^2}-|\\bar u|^2\\), \\(V_q=\\overline{q^2}-\\bar q^2\\), the desired inequality follows from\n\\[\ne_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma+\n\\frac{(c_a-p_a-b_a)^2+(c_d-p_d-b_d)^2}{4\\lambda}\n+t_+(p_a+b_a)&lt;(1+t_+)f_*, \\tag{s0}\n\\]\nor from the same inequality with \\(f_*+V_q\\) on the right.</p><p>**Short widths \\(0&lt;h\\le.16\\).**\nPut \\(G=r^2w^2\\), \\(u_0=Gh^2/3\\). Lemma 4.1 gives\n\\(W\\le1.507\\), \\(\\int W\\le1.012\\), and Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\ne_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma\\le.54u_0.\n\\]\nThe remaining estimates are\n\\[\n\\frac{f_*}{u_0}\\ge\n\\frac{1-2w^4h^2/(15(1+w^2))}{1+h^2/3},\\qquad\n|p_d|\\le2hu_0,\n\\]\n\\[\np_a/u_0\\le4+.15h^2,\\qquad\nb_a/u_0\\le.193^2(4+.15h^2)/G,\n\\]\n\\[\n|c_a|/u_0\\le.79(1.012)/G,\\qquad\n|c_d|\\le.247h,\n\\]\n\\[\n|b_d|\\le2(.193)h\\cdot.53(1.012)h^2/3.\n\\]\nThey follow from the Taylor bounds for \\(\\sin^2\\), \\(\\sinh^2\\), the inequalities\n\\[\n|\\sin z-z\\cos z|\\le|z|^3/3,\\qquad\n0\\le\\coth h-1/h\\le h/3,\n\\]\nand the physical averaging moment\n\\[\n\\overline{\\beta^2}\\le1/3+.05h^2\\quad(h\\le.54).\n\\]\nSubstitution with \\(H=.16\\), \\(P=4+.15H^2\\), \\(Q=.193^2P/G\\) bounds the normalized remaining budget below by\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;(1+t_+)\\frac{1-2w^4H^2/(15(1+w^2))}{1+H^2/3}\n-t_+(P+Q)\\\\\n&amp;-\\frac1{4\\lambda}\\left[\n\\frac{GH^2}{3}(P+Q+.79(1.012)/G)^2\n+\\frac3G\\left(.247+\\frac23(G+.193(.53)(1.012))H^2\\right)^2\n\\right]&gt;.55510&gt;.54.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>**Tail endpoint cases, \\(h\\ge.16\\).**\nIf \\(|l|&lt;1.4\\), \\(y\\ge3.6\\), then \\(h\\ge1.1\\) and\n\\[\nf_*\\ge.0449,\\quad .0818\\le p_a\\le.1074,\\quad |p_d|\\le.0132.\n\\]\nIf \\(l\\le-1.4\\), then \\(h\\ge1.4\\), and\n\\[\nf_*\\ge.0456,\\quad .0830\\le p_a\\le.1062,\\quad |p_d|\\le.0129.\n\\]\nThese follow by diagonalizing the elementary quadratic forms:\n\\[\n\\frac{1-\\sqrt{1+w^2\\coth^2h}}{2(1+w^2)}\n\\le A_h\\cos(wh)\\le\n\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1+w^2\\coth^2h}}{2(1+w^2)},\n\\]\n\\[\n|B_h\\sin(wh)|\\le\n\\frac{\\coth h+\\sqrt{\\coth^2h+w^2}}{2(1+w^2)}.\n\\]</p><p>In the central/distant case, \\(x_+&gt;65.8\\), \\(|x_-|&lt;6.86\\), and the positive-part integral bounds imply \\(\\overline{\\mathsf K}&lt;.0084\\).\nSeparating \\(|x|&lt;5\\) gives \\(b_+&lt;.0068\\), while \\(b_-&lt;.032\\).\nHere\n\\[\nc_a\\in[-.081,.160],\\quad |c_d|\\le.080,\\quad\ne_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma\\le.0144.\n\\]\nFor opposite tails,\n\\[\nb_\\pm&lt;.032,\\quad c_a\\in[-.006,.160],\\quad |c_d|\\le.003,\\quad\ne_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma\\le.0277.\n\\]\nThe last two bounds are obtained by maximizing the convex endpoint expression over the \\(\\mathsf K_\\pm\\) rectangles\n\\[\n[-.007,.0275]\\times[0,.0008],\\qquad\n[-.0001,.0191]^2,\n\\]\nwith \\(|\\mathsf C_+-\\mathsf C_-|\\le.160,.006\\), respectively.\nThe remaining budgets are\n\\[\n1.064(.0449)-\\frac{.2078^2+.1092^2}{2.6}\n-.064(.1074+.0194)&gt;.018&gt;.0144,\n\\]\n\\[\n1.064(.0456)-\\frac{.1442^2+.0319^2}{2.6}\n-.064(.1062+.032)&gt;.031&gt;.0277.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(l\\ge1.4\\), then convexity gives \\(e_{\\mathsf K}\\le0\\), and\n\\[\n\\Gamma/f_*\\le.10,\\quad\np_a+|p_d|\\le\\min(.150,6.65f_*),\\quad f_*\\ge.008.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(|\\overline{\\mathsf C}-\\mathsf C_\\pm|\\le.006\\) and\n\\(b_\\pm\\le.00045\\), the left side of (s0), divided by \\(f_*\\), is at most\n\\[\n.10+\\frac1{4\\lambda}\n\\left(\\sqrt{.150\\cdot6.65}+\\frac{.00645}{\\sqrt{.008}}\\right)^2\n+t_+(6.65+.00045/.008)&lt;1+t_+.\n\\]</p><p>**Weighted chord consequences in the bounded region.**\nThe remaining region is\n\\[\n|l|\\le1.4,\\qquad y\\le3.6.\n\\]\nThe layer-cake identity for \\(1-\\beta^2\\), combined with Lemma 4.2, yields\n\\[\n-c_a\\le h^2g_hL_C(h_s')/3,\\qquad\nh_s'=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\min(h,T_0),&amp;l\\ge0,\\\\\nh/2,&amp;l&lt;0,\\ h/2\\le T_0.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{s2}\n\\]\nFor \\(l\\ge0\\), \\(.16\\le h\\le T_0\\), put\n\\[\nd_s=\\alpha_D-\\beta_Dh,\\qquad b_s=\\alpha_E-\\beta_Eh.\n\\]\nThe same argument and endpoint concentration give\n\\[\ne_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma\n\\le\\frac{h^2}{3}\n \\{g_hL_N(h)+12d_{\\mathsf K}d_s^2+12d_{\\mathsf C}b_s^2\\}. \\tag{s3}\n\\]\nFor \\(h\\le\\pi/w\\), the angular half-difference is nonpositive and\n\\[\n|c_d-p_d|\\le.08;\n\\]\nwhen \\(l\\ge0\\), \\(.16\\le h\\le T_0\\), it sharpens to \\(|c_d-p_d|\\le hb_s\\).</p><p>**Middle widths \\(.16\\le h\\le.54\\).**\nOne has \\(p_a/h^2&gt;.33\\), and hence \\(c_a&lt;p_a\\).\nFor \\(l&lt;0\\), put\n\\[\nB_a^*=(.193)^2(4/3+.05h^2),\\qquad B_d^*=.078h^3.\n\\]\nIt suffices that\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1+t_+)f_*&gt;&amp;\\ .0584h^2+.00025+t_+(p_a+h^2B_a^*)\\\\\n&amp;+\\frac{(p_a+h^2(g_hL_C(h/2)/3+B_a^*))^2\n           +(.08+B_d^*)^2}{4\\lambda}. \\qquad(s4)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(l\\ge0\\), define\n\\[\na_s=p_a/h^2+g_hL_C(h)/3,\\quad\nz=\\frac{|q_+-q_-|}{2h}\\le.193,\\quad\nv_h=h\\coth h-h.\n\\]\nThe physical averaging density is at least \\(v_h/(2h)\\). The derivative bound \\(|\\dot q|\\le.193\\), applied to the test \\((q-\\bar q)^2\\), gives\n\\[\nV_q/h^2\\ge v_hz^3/(3\\cdot.193).\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n0\\le b_a-V_q\\le h^2(z^2+h^2/25),\\qquad\n|b_d|\\le2h^3z/5.\n\\]\nExpanding the squared costs and retaining the variance gives the remainder\n\\[\nR=Q_s(z^2+h^2/25)+h^2b_sz/(5\\lambda)+h^4z^2/(25\\lambda)\n-\\frac{.855}{3\\cdot.193}v_hz^3,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_s=t_++h^2(2a_s+.05)/(4\\lambda).\n\\]\nHere \\(h^2a_s\\le.16025\\), so\n\\[\n1-\\frac{h^2a_s+.0163}{2\\lambda}&gt;.855.\n\\]</p><p>To verify \\(R/h^2\\le.027\\), set \\(Z=z/h\\). On successive width intervals with endpoints\n\\[\n.16,.23,.29,.35,.40,.45,.50,.54,\n\\]\nbound it by the maximum of \\(Q/25+AZ^2+BZ-N_0Z^3\\), \\(Z\\ge0\\).\nThe following coefficients are scaled by \\(10^5\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{rrrrrr}\nh^2a_s&amp;Q&amp;A&amp;B&amp;N_0&amp;\\sup(R/h^2)\\\\\n8298&amp;12885&amp;12902&amp;1576&amp;20047&amp;2028\\\\\n13121&amp;16655&amp;16699&amp;1851&amp;26748&amp;2449\\\\\n15242&amp;18360&amp;18453&amp;2069&amp;31598&amp;2526\\\\\n15218&amp;18414&amp;18572&amp;2208&amp;35687&amp;2308\\\\\n15449&amp;18673&amp;18925&amp;2309&amp;38555&amp;2242\\\\\n15768&amp;19010&amp;19395&amp;2370&amp;40970&amp;2218\\\\\n16025&amp;19288&amp;19811&amp;2390&amp;42963&amp;2195\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThese follow from\n\\[\nS_u=1+h^2/6+.0084h^4,\\quad\nH_u=1+h^2/2+.0421h^4,\\quad\ng_u=.75S_u(1+H_u),\n\\]\nand, for an interval \\([L,H]\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_0={}&amp;g_u(H)H^2L_C(H)/3\\\\\n&amp;+\\min\\left\\{\nr^2w^2H^2(4/3+.05H^2),\n2r^2\\left[1-\\frac{1-\\sqrt{1+w^2(1/L+L/3)^2}}{2(1+w^2)}\\right]\n\\right\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\\[\nQ=t_++(2A_0+.05H^2)/(4\\lambda),\\quad\nA=Q+H^4/(25\\lambda),\\quad\nB=H(\\alpha_E-\\beta_EH)/(5\\lambda),\n\\]\n\\[\nN_0=\\frac{.855L(1-L+L^2/3-.023L^4)}{3\\cdot.193}.\n\\]\nThe cubic maximum occurs at\n\\[\nZ_*=\\frac{A+\\sqrt{A^2+3N_0B}}{3N_0}\n\\]\nand equals \\(Q/25+(AZ_*^2+2BZ_*)/3\\).\nThus the nonnegative-endpoint case reduces to\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1+t_+)f_*/h^2&gt;&amp;\\\n\\{g_hL_N(h)+12d_{\\mathsf K}d_s^2+12d_{\\mathsf C}b_s^2\\}/3\\\\\n&amp;+(h^2a_s^2+b_s^2)/(4\\lambda)+t_+p_a/h^2+.027h^2. \\qquad(s5)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>For completeness, the paper verifies (s4) and (s5) by exact polynomial certificates. Let ordinary convolution be denoted by \\(*\\), and put\n\\[\ns_i=\\frac1{(2i+1)!},\\quad a_i=\\frac1{(2i)!},\\quad\nd_i=\\frac{(-4w^2)^i}{(2i+1)!},\\quad\ne_i=\\frac{2(-4w^2)^i}{(2i+2)!}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\np_aS/h^2\\le P_u\n=.00025+\\frac{2r^2w^2}{1+w^2}\n\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^7(s_i-(a*d)_i)h^{2i-2}\n+\\sum_{i=0}^7(s*e)_ih^{2i}\\right],\n\\]\n\\[\nf_*S^2/h^2\\ge F_*=\n\\frac{r^2w^2}{1+w^2}\n\\sum_{i=1}^6\n\\frac{2\\cdot4^i(1-(-w^2)^i)}{(2i+2)!}h^{2i-2}.\n\\]\nThe omitted series for \\(F_*\\) alternates with first term positive and decreasing magnitude; for \\(P_u\\), the omitted absolute tail is below \\(.00025\\).</p><p>After multiplying the deficits by \\(S^2\\), lower bounds are\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{(4)}={}&amp;(1+t_+)h^2F_*\n-S_u^2\\{\\ .0584h^2+.00025+t_+h^2B_a^*\n+(.08+B_d^*)^2/(4\\lambda)\\}\\\\\n&amp;-t_+h^2P_uS_u\n-\\frac{h^4}{4\\lambda}\n \\{P_u+S_u(g_uL_C(h/2)/3+B_a^*)\\}^2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{(5)}={}&amp;(1+t_+)F_*\n-S_u^2\\left[\n\\{g_uL_N(h)+12d_{\\mathsf K}d_s^2+12d_{\\mathsf C}b_s^2\\}/3\n+b_s^2/(4\\lambda)+.027h^2\\right]\\\\\n&amp;-\\frac{h^2}{4\\lambda}\\{P_u+S_ug_uL_C(h)/3\\}^2-t_+P_uS_u.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTheir degrees are \\(32,30\\). Under \\(h=.16+.38u\\), their Bernstein coefficients, multiplied by \\(10^5\\), have grouped lower bounds\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\n&amp;0:7&amp;8:15&amp;16:23&amp;24:\\deg\\\\ \\hline\nT_{(4)}&amp;173&amp;576&amp;770&amp;412\\\\\nT_{(5)}&amp;1586&amp;701&amp;408&amp;407\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand are all positive. Explicitly, if \\(t_j=[h^j]T\\), the coefficient of index \\(r\\) is\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^r\n\\frac{\\binom ri}{\\binom{\\deg T}{i}}(.38)^i\n\\sum_{j=i}^{\\deg T}t_j\\binom ji(.16)^{j-i}.\n\\]\nThese finite rational sums prove positivity on the whole interval.</p><p>**Larger widths \\(h\\ge.54\\).**\nFor \\(.54\\le h\\le\\pi/w\\), \\(p_a\\) decreases and \\(f_*\\) increases. Use\n\\[\n-c_a\\le.0802,\\quad c_a\\le.160,\\quad b_a\\le b_M=.0163.\n\\]\nOn successive intervals with endpoints \\(.54,.58,.63,.684\\), define\n\\[\nR_P=\\min(.0802,H^2g_HL_C(.54)/3),\\qquad\nR_O=\\min(.0802,H^2g_HL_C(H/2)/3).\n\\]\nBounds scaled by \\(10^5\\) are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{rrrrrrr}\np_a&amp;f_*&amp;U_P&amp;R_P&amp;R_O&amp;\\text{budget }P&amp;\\text{budget }O\\\\\n11379&amp;4367&amp;2004&amp;5141&amp;7101&amp;2190&amp;1901\\\\\n10895&amp;4500&amp;1970&amp;6216&amp;8020&amp;2278&amp;2006\\\\\n10112&amp;4593&amp;1937&amp;7539&amp;8020&amp;2348&amp;2276\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nwhere \\(U_P=.01815+1/(312\\sinh^2(2L))\\), while the opposite-sign primitive cost is \\(.01815\\). The budgets are\n\\[\n(1+t_+)f_*-t_+(p_a+b_M)\n-\\{(R_j+p_a+b_M)^2+(.08+b_M)^2\\}/(4\\lambda).\n\\]\nTaylor polynomials through degree \\(20\\), with absolute errors below \\(10^{-8}\\), certify the entries.</p><p>For \\(h\\ge\\pi/w&gt;.68\\), the angular range gives \\(p_a&gt;.077\\).\nIf the angular half-difference has the favorable sign, \\(|c_d-p_d|\\le.08\\).\nOtherwise its magnitude improves to \\(.00810\\), giving\n\\(|c_d-p_d|\\le.08810\\), while \\(p_a\\le.0968\\).</p><p>For \\(\\pi/w\\le h\\le.9\\), \\(p_a\\le.0968\\), \\(f_*\\ge.0431\\), and the remaining budget is at least \\(.02005\\), exceeding the primitive cost \\(.01912\\).\nFor \\(h\\ge.9\\), \\(f_*\\ge.04413\\); the two sign cases give\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{cc|cc}\np_a\\text{ upper bound}&amp;|c_d-p_d|\\text{ upper bound}\n&amp;\\text{budget}&amp;\\text{cost}\\\\ \\hline\n.0968&amp;.08810&amp;.02115&amp;.01853\\\\\n.10882&amp;.08&amp;.01916&amp;.01853\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nwith strict margins.</p><p>The cases cover every \\(h&gt;0\\), including shared boundaries. All budgets are strict, proving (4). At coincident endpoints every deviation vanishes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Chord kernel — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Chord kernel — paper: Lemma B.2.**</p><p>Let the endpoint \\(t\\)-coordinates be \\(m-h,m+h\\), \\(h&gt;0\\). For \\(t=m+h\\beta\\), define\n\\[\n\\theta=\\frac{\\sinh t-\\sinh(m-h)}{2\\cosh m\\sinh h},\\qquad\nS=\\frac{\\sinh h}{h},\\qquad\ng_h=\\frac34S(1+\\cosh h).\n\\]\nFor every twice continuously differentiable profile \\(F\\),\n\\[\n\\bar F-\\frac{F_-+F_+}{2}\n=-\\frac{h^2}{3}\\int_{-1}^1W(\\beta)n_F(m+h\\beta)\\,\\frac{d\\beta}{2},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nW=6\\theta(1-\\theta)\\frac{\\cosh m\\sinh h}{\\cosh t\\,h}.\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n0\\le W\\le\\frac32S,\\qquad\nW\\le g_h(1-\\beta^2),\\qquad\n\\int_{-1}^1W\\,\\frac{d\\beta}{2}\\le\\frac S2(1+\\cosh h).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The uniform trapezoidal error in \\(x\\) is\n\\[\n-\\frac{(x_+-x_-)^2}{2}\n \\int_0^1\\theta(1-\\theta)F''(x_-+\\theta(x_+-x_-))\\,d\\theta.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(x_+-x_-=2s\\cosh m\\sinh h\\) and\n\\(d\\theta=h\\cosh t\\,d\\beta/(2\\cosh m\\sinh h)\\).</p><p>The first upper bound reduces to\n\\[\n(\\sinh t-\\sinh m\\cosh h)^2\n\\ge\\cosh m(\\cosh m-\\cosh t)\\sinh^2h.\n\\]\nWith \\(\\zeta=\\cosh h-1\\), the difference is\n\\[\n(\\sinh t-\\sinh m)^2+\n2(\\cosh(m-t)-1)\\zeta+\n(\\cosh t\\cosh m-1)\\zeta^2\\ge0.\n\\]</p><p>For the endpoint-sensitive bound, put \\(u_\\pm=(1\\pm\\beta)/2\\), factor the hyperbolic-sine differences, and obtain\n\\[\n\\theta(1-\\theta)\\frac{\\cosh m}{\\cosh t}\n=\\frac{\\sinh(u_+h)\\sinh(u_-h)}{\\sinh^2h}\n \\frac{\\cosh(2m+\\betah)+\\cosh h}\n      {\\cosh(2m+\\betah)+\\cosh(\\betah)}.\n\\]\nConvexity gives \\(\\sinh(u_\\pm h)\\le u_\\pm\\sinh h\\), and the quotient is at most \\((1+\\cosh h)/2\\). Integration uses\n\\(\\int_{-1}^1(1-\\beta^2)d\\beta/2=2/3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Concentration and primitive bounds — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Concentration and primitive bounds — paper: Lemma B.3.**</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\nm\\ge0,\\quad l=m-h\\in[-1.4,1.4],\\quad y=m+h\\le3.6,\\quad y\\ge|l|.\n\\]\nPut \\(T_0=.54\\),\n\\[\nD_0=\\mathsf K+t_c\\mathsf C,\\qquad\nd_{\\mathsf K}=\\frac{.80\\cdot1.25}{m_*^2},\\qquad\nd_{\\mathsf C}=\\frac{.80\\cdot5t_r^2}{m_*^2}.\n\\]\nFor the four profiles \\(A_j\\), use\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\nj&amp;A_j&amp;\\alpha_j&amp;\\beta_j\\\\ \\hline\nD&amp;\\dot D_0&amp;.07052&amp;.0754\\\\\nE&amp;-\\dot{\\mathsf C}&amp;.281&amp;.254\\\\\nN&amp;-n_{\\mathsf K}&amp;.24&amp;.277\\\\\nC&amp;n_{\\mathsf C}&amp;.728&amp;.812\n\\end{array}\n\\qquad\nL_j(H)=2\\alpha_j/3-\\beta_jH/2.\n\\]\nOn every measurable subset of \\([0,3.6]\\) of length \\(2H\\), \\(0&lt;H\\le T_0\\), the mean of \\((A_j)_+\\) is at most \\(\\alpha_j-\\beta_jH\\). The same bound holds for a density between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) of mass \\(2H\\).</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\n\\dot D_0\\ge-.022,\n\\]\nand, for \\(0\\le t\\le t'\\le3.6\\),\n\\[\n\\mathsf C(t')-\\mathsf C(t)\\le.000124,\\qquad\nq(t)-q(t')\\le.000084.\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\nb_a=\\frac12\\left(\\overline{(q-q_+)^2}+\n                         \\overline{(q-q_-)^2}\\right),\n\\]\nthen \\(b_a\\le.0163\\). Finally,\n\\[\nl&lt;0:\\quad e_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma\n\\le\\min(.018,.0146\\cdot4h^2+.00010)+.00015,\n\\]\n\\[\nl\\ge0:\\quad e_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma\n\\le.01815+\\frac1{312\\sinh^2(2h)}. \\tag{s1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(I_j(v)=\\int_0^{3.6}(A_j-v)_+\\,dt\\), thresholding gives\n\\[\n\\int\\vartheta(A_j)_+\\le2Hv+I_j(v)\n\\]\nwhen \\(0\\le\\vartheta\\le1\\), \\(\\int\\vartheta=2H\\).\nFor successive intervals of \\(1000H\\) with endpoints\n\\[\n0,75,150,200,250,300,350,400,540,\n\\]\nthe following entries give \\(1000v\\), then upper bounds for \\(10^5I_j(v)\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrr}\nD&amp;61&amp;54&amp;44&amp;37&amp;29&amp;21&amp;14&amp;0\\\\\nE&amp;247&amp;224&amp;192&amp;167&amp;141&amp;116&amp;90&amp;42\\\\\nN&amp;216&amp;178&amp;143&amp;115&amp;77&amp;42&amp;17&amp;0\\\\\nC&amp;664&amp;545&amp;444&amp;347&amp;234&amp;152&amp;97&amp;21\\\\ \\hline\nD&amp;0&amp;105&amp;419&amp;716&amp;1124&amp;1598&amp;2069&amp;3167\\\\\nE&amp;0&amp;351&amp;1326&amp;2363&amp;3673&amp;5133&amp;6885&amp;10779\\\\\nN&amp;0&amp;577&amp;1576&amp;2624&amp;4378&amp;6352&amp;8026&amp;9323\\\\\nC&amp;0&amp;1776&amp;4528&amp;8066&amp;13250&amp;17831&amp;21389&amp;27670\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nAt each interval endpoint these bounds are at most\n\\(2H(\\alpha_j-\\beta_jH-v)\\); concavity supplies the interval.\nThe rigorous integral bounds are the finite sums\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{144}\\frac{\\delta_i}{80}\n  (A_j(t_i)+\\varepsilon_j-v)_+,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\delta_0=\\delta_{144}=1\\), otherwise \\(\\delta_i=2\\), and\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_D=.00040,\\quad\\varepsilon_E=.00120,\\quad\n\\varepsilon_N=.00217,\\quad\\varepsilon_C=.00741.\n\\]\nThese are justified by the chord errors of Proposition 2.</p><p>For a profile \\(F\\), define its physical primitive average\n\\[\nP_F(t)=\\frac{\\int_0^1F(ts)\\cosh(ts)\\,ds}\n                 {\\int_0^1\\cosh(ts)\\,ds}.\n\\]\nIts starred samples are\n\\[\nP_{F,i}^*=F_i-\n40\\sum_{k=1}^i\n \\frac{\\cosh t_k-\\cosh t_{k-1}}{\\sinh t_i}(F_k-F_{k-1}),\n\\qquad P_{F,0}^*=F_0.\n\\]\nThis exactly integrates the piecewise affine interpolant. The total chord errors for\n\\(P_{\\mathsf K},P_q,P_{q^2}\\) are respectively\n\\[\n.000051,\\quad.000128,\\quad.000076.\n\\]\nIndeed, if \\(G_F=\\sup|\\ddot F|\\), \\(L_F=\\sup|\\dot F|\\), \\(o_F=\\operatorname{osc}F\\), then\n\\[\n|\\ddot P_F|\\le G_F+2L_F+o_F,\n\\]\nand the total error is at most \\((2G_F+2L_F+o_F)/12800\\).</p><p>Write \\(P=P_{\\mathsf K}\\), \\(q_M=.2559\\), and define\n\\[\nB_0(t)=P(t)-(\\mathsf K(t)+\\mathsf K(0))/2\n+d_{\\mathsf K}(D_0(t)-D_0(0))^2\n+d_{\\mathsf C}(\\mathsf C(t)-\\mathsf C(0))^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nB_1(t)=-\\tfrac12(\\mathsf K(t)-\\mathsf K(0))\n+d_{\\mathsf K}(D_0(t)-D_0(0))_+\n             (D_0(t)+D_0(0)+.0214)_+,\n\\]\n\\[\nZ_1(t)=\\tanh t(.0178-P(t)).\n\\]\nThe finite rational sample rules of Lemma 2.2 give\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|l|l}\ni&amp;\\text{expression}&amp;\\text{bound}\\\\ \\hline\n0:144&amp;D_0-D_0(0)&amp;[-10^{-8},.0314]\\\\\n&amp;P&amp;\\le.01767\\\\\n&amp;B_0&amp;\\le.01773\\\\\n&amp;B_0-.0146t_i^2&amp;\\le.00001\\\\\n&amp;\\dot D_0&amp;\\ge-.0213\\\\\n0:56&amp;B_1+\\frac12(P-.0049)_+&amp;\\le.000001\\\\\n&amp;Z_1&amp;[0,.00742]\\\\\n&amp;B_1+78Z_1^2&amp;\\le.000001\\\\\n&amp;P_{q^2}-(q+q_M)P_q+(q^2+q_M^2)/2&amp;\\le.01587\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand the sampled columns\n\\[\n-\\mathsf C,\\quad q,\\quad\\min(D_0,-.01065),\\quad\\min(P,.0049)\n\\]\nare nondecreasing. For the first two, successive differences are at least\n\\(.000005,.000012\\); clipped columns use exact saturated constants.</p><p>The chord errors imply almost-monotonicity and\n\\[\nP(y)\\ge\\min(P(t),.0049)-2(.000051),\\quad P\\le.0178,\n\\]\n\\[\nD_0(y)\\ge\\min(D_0(t),-.01065)-.000040.\n\\]\nConvexity in the profile arguments gives\n\\[\nB_0(t)\\le\\min(.018,.0146t^2+.00010).\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(t=|l|\\). Primitive subtraction gives\n\\[\n\\overline{\\mathsf K}=P(y)+\\theta(P(y)-P(t)),\n\\qquad\n\\theta=\\frac{\\sinh l}{\\sinh y-\\sinh l}.\n\\]\nAfter subtracting \\(B_0(y)\\), the common correction is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;-\\tfrac12(\\mathsf K(t)-\\mathsf K(0))\\\\\n&amp;+d_{\\mathsf K}(D_0(t)-D_0(0))\n       (D_0(t)+D_0(0)-2D_0(y))\\\\\n&amp;+d_{\\mathsf C}(\\mathsf C(t)-\\mathsf C(0))\n       (\\mathsf C(t)+\\mathsf C(0)-2\\mathsf C(y)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe clipped bounds and almost-monotonicity bound this by \\(B_1(t)+.000014\\).\nThe chord error of \\(B_1\\) is below \\(.000020\\).</p><p>If \\(l&lt;0\\), then \\(-1/2\\le\\theta\\le0\\); using the sample bound for\n\\(B_1+\\frac12(P-.0049)_+\\), the total correction is below\n\\[\n.000014+.000020+.000001+.000051/2+.000051&lt;.00015.\n\\]\nSince \\(y\\le2h\\), the first part of (s1) follows.</p><p>If \\(l\\ge0\\), then\n\\[\n0\\le\\theta\\le\\tanh l/\\sinh(2h).\n\\]\nThe chord error of \\(Z_1\\) is below \\(.000066\\). Thus the sample bound for\n\\(B_1+78Z_1^2\\) gives\n\\[\ne_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma-B_0(y)\n\\le.00015+\\frac{Z_1(t)}{\\sinh(2h)}-78Z_1(t)^2.\n\\]\nMaximizing the last two terms gives \\(1/(312\\sinh^2(2h))\\).</p><p>Finally, on \\([|l|,y]\\), almost-monotonicity of \\(q\\) bounds the pointwise half-sum of squared endpoint distances by\n\\[\n.1784^2/2+.000084(.1784+.000084)&lt;.0163.\n\\]\nIf \\(l&lt;0\\), reflect \\([l,|l|]\\) and maximize the convex quadratic in the other endpoint value. The resulting upper bound is\n\\[\nP_{q^2}(t)-(q(t)+q_M)P_q(t)\n+\\frac{q(t)^2+q_M^2}{2}+\\frac{.000084^2}{2}.\n\\]\nIts upper error is below \\(.00018\\), so the displayed sample bound proves \\(b_a\\le.0163\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Biased projections, tails, and the Gaussian Sobolev interface — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Biased projections, tails, and the Gaussian Sobolev interface — paper: Lemmas 3.1–3.3.**</p><p>Let \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\) be closed, convex, solid, and pointed; let \\(D=C^*\\), \\(U\\in\\operatorname{int}C\\), \\(V\\in\\operatorname{int}D\\), with \\(\\langle U,V\\rangle=m\\).\nLet \\(T\\) be deterministic symmetric with \\(t_-I\\le T\\le t_+I\\), and put\n\\[\n\\Sigma=I+T,\\qquad Z=\\Sigma^{1/2}G,\\qquad G\\sim N(0,I_m).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\na(z)=\\phi(z)+zp(z).\n\\]\nThere is a unique smooth bias curve \\(\\xi_z\\) such that\n\\[\nX_z=\\Pi_C(Z+\\xi_z),\\qquad\nY_z=\\Pi_D(-Z-\\xi_z),\\qquad\n\\mathbb EX_z=a(z)U. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nWrite \\(P_z=D\\Pi_C(Z+\\xi_z)\\) almost everywhere.</p><p>For symmetric matrix fields use\n\\[\n\\tau B=\\frac1m\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}B,\\quad\n\\tau_\\Lambda B=\\tau(\\Lambda B),\\quad\n\\langle B,E\\rangle_\\Lambda=\\tau_\\Lambda(B\\circ E),\\quad\nB\\circ E=(BE+EB)/2.\n\\]\nFor arbitrary square fields set \\(\\|B\\|_2^2=\\tau(B^TB)\\).</p><p>For a smooth scalar test \\(f\\) whose derivatives have polynomial growth, define\n\\[\nH_f=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(p(z)I-P_z)f'(z)\\,dz,\\qquad\n\\iota(x)=x,\n\\]\n\\[\nA=H_\\iota,\\qquad M_i=\\mathbb E(G_iA),\\qquad\nL=\\sum_{i=1}^mG_iM_i,\\qquad R=A-L. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nThese integrals converge in every finite \\(L^r\\), and the displayed fields have all finite moments. The centered vector field\n\\[\nW_f=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(p(z)Z-X_z+\\mathbb EX_z)f'(z)\\,dz\n\\]\nbelongs to Gaussian \\(W^{1,2}\\), with weak Jacobian\n\\[\nD_GW_f=H_f\\Sigma^{1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 constructs the biased field and its identities. Lemma 5.2 proves convergence of all polynomially weighted layer integrals and all finite moments, including those defining \\(H_f,A,M_i,L,R\\). Lemma 5.3 supplies the Sobolev assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Biased projections — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Biased projections — paper: Lemma 3.1.**</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 5, the bias exists uniquely and smoothly, \\(P_z\\) is a symmetric positive contraction, and\n\\[\nX_z-Y_z=Z+\\xi_z,\\qquad X_z\\cdot Y_z=0,\\qquad\n(\\mathbb EP_z)\\xi_z'=p(z)U.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nB(z)=m^{-1}U\\cdot\\mathbb EY_z,\\quad\nr_1(z)=-B'(z),\\quad\nh(z)=\\tau_\\Sigma P_z,\\quad s_0=\\tau\\Sigma.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nh(z)=m^{-1}\\mathbb E(X_z\\cdot Z)\n=m^{-1}\\mathbb E|X_z-\\mathbb EX_z|^2+a(z)B(z), \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\\[\nh'=pB+ar_1,\\qquad r_1&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Moreau decomposition in the positive-dual convention gives\n\\[\n\\Pi_C(w)-\\Pi_D(-w)=w\n\\]\nand orthogonality. Directly, if \\(x=\\Pi_C(w)\\), the projection inequality gives\n\\(\\langle w-x,x\\rangle=0\\) and \\(y=x-w\\in D\\); the dual projection inequality gives \\(y=\\Pi_D(-w)\\).</p><p>The projection is one-Lipschitz and is the gradient of\n\\[\n\\Phi(w)=\\tfrac12|\\Pi_C(w)|^2\n=\\sup_{x\\in C}\\{\\langle w,x\\rangle-\\tfrac12|x|^2\\}.\n\\]\nRademacher’s theorem and the convex-gradient representation imply that its almost-everywhere derivative is symmetric and lies between \\(0\\) and \\(I\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(a&gt;0\\), minimize\n\\[\n\\Psi_a(\\xi)=\\mathbb E\\Phi(Z+\\xi)-aU\\cdot\\xi.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\xi=x-y\\) by cone decomposition. Interiority gives \\(U\\cdot y\\ge c|y|\\), and Jensen gives\n\\[\n\\Psi_a(\\xi)\\ge\\tfrac12|x|^2-a|U||x|+ac|y|.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\Psi_a\\) is coercive. Gaussian convolution makes it smooth; its Hessian is \\(\\mathbb ED\\Pi_C(Z+\\xi)\\), strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(I\\), because the Gaussian sees open subsets of \\(\\operatorname{int}C\\) and \\(-\\operatorname{int}D\\). The unique minimizer satisfies the required mean condition. The implicit function theorem gives smoothness and the derivative formula.</p><p>Choose a jointly measurable bounded contraction representative of the derivative; layerwise Gaussian-null exceptional sets are harmless by Fubini.</p><p>Gaussian integration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(X_z\\cdot Z)=\\mathbb E\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma P_z).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbb EY_z=aU-\\xi_z\\), orthogonality gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(X_z\\cdot Z)=\\mathbb E|X_z|^2-aU\\cdot\\xi_z,\n\\]\nproving (6). Also\n\\[\n\\frac d{dz}\\mathbb E|X_z|^2=2aU\\cdot\\xi_z',\n\\]\nwhich yields \\(h'=pB+ar_1\\), and\n\\[\nr_1=\\frac p m U\\cdot\\bigl((\\mathbb EP_z)^{-1}-I\\bigr)U&gt;0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Gaussian layer tails — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Gaussian layer tails — paper: Lemma 3.2.**</p><p>For each finite \\(r\\ge1\\), there are \\(c_r,C_r&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|P_z\\|_{L^r}+\\|X_z\\|_{L^r}\\le C_re^{-c_rz^2}\\quad(z\\le0),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|I-P_z\\|_{L^r}+\\|Y_z\\|_{L^r}\\le C_re^{-c_rz^2}\\quad(z\\ge0).\n\\]\nThe constants are locally uniform under invertible linear changes of the fixed cone data and uniform over the spectral box for \\(T\\).\nAlso \\(B(z)\\le C(1+|z|)\\) for \\(z\\le0\\).\nThe positive measure \\(\\mu=h'(z)\\,dz\\) has mass \\(s_0\\) and all polynomial moments, as do the measures with densities \\(ar_1\\) and \\(pr_1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Interiority gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|X_z|\\le Ca(z),\\qquad \\mathbb E|Y_z|\\le CB(z).\n\\]\nFor any one-Lipschitz vector function,\n\\[\n|F(Z)|\\le\\mathbb E|F(Z)|+\\mathbb E|Z|+|Z|.\n\\]\nFor \\(s\\ge0\\),\n\\[\na(-s)\\asymp\\frac{\\phi(s)}{(1+s)^2},\\qquad\np(-s)\\le C\\frac{\\phi(s)}{1+s},\n\\]\nand \\(a(z)\\asymp1+z\\) on \\(z\\ge0\\).</p><p>For negative \\(z\\), split \\(\\mathbb E(X_z\\cdot Z)\\) at\n\\(|Z|=C_0(1+|z|)\\). The first piece and Gaussian tail estimate give\n\\[\n0\\le h(z)\\le C(1+|z|)a(z),\\qquad B(z)\\le C(1+|z|).\n\\]\nSince \\(0\\le P_z\\le I\\), \\(\\Sigma\\ge.978I\\), all finite moments of \\(P_z\\) decay Gaussianly. Interpolation between the small first moment of \\(X_z\\) and bounded higher moments gives the \\(X_z\\) estimate.</p><p>For positive \\(z\\), \\(aB\\le h\\le s_0\\), so\n\\[\n|\\mathbb EY_z|\\le Cs_0/a(z),\\qquad\n\\xi_z=a(z)U-\\mathbb EY_z.\n\\]\nAn interior ball about \\(U\\) shows that \\(\\xi_z\\) has distance at least \\(cz\\) from the complement of \\(C\\) for large \\(z\\). On \\(|Z|&lt;cz\\), \\(Y_z=0\\) and \\(P_z=I\\) almost surely. Gaussian tails prove the remaining bounds.</p><p>The monotone function \\(h\\) tends to \\(0,s_0\\) with Gaussian tails, proving the assertions for \\(\\mu\\). Finally \\(ar_1\\le h'\\), and \\(p/a\\) has polynomial growth.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Gaussian Sobolev interface — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Gaussian Sobolev interface — paper: Lemma 3.3.**</p><p>For every scalar test \\(f\\) allowed in Proposition 5, \\(W_f\\) converges in Gaussian \\(W^{1,2}\\) and\n\\[\nD_GW_f=H_f\\Sigma^{1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At negative layers the integrand decays in \\(L^2\\) at a Gaussian rate. At positive layers use\n\\[\nX_z-\\mathbb EX_z=Z+Y_z-\\mathbb EY_z\n\\]\nto obtain the same decay. Its weak Jacobian is\n\\[\n(p(z)I-P_z)\\Sigma^{1/2},\n\\]\nwhose \\(L^2\\) norm has integrable Gaussian tails. Truncated integrals and their derivatives are Cauchy in \\(L^2\\); closedness of the weak derivative gives the result. Each integrand is centered. Only the Lipschitz projection is differentiated, not its derivative field.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Simultaneous normalization — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous normalization — paper: Proposition 3.4.**</p><p>For the cone data in Proposition 5, one can make an invertible linear change\n\\[\n(C,D,U,V)\\mapsto(BC,B^{-T}D,BU,B^{-T}V)\n\\]\nand choose a deterministic symmetric \\(T\\), \\(t_-I\\le T\\le t_+I\\), so that the associated fields satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathbb EA=0,\\qquad\n\\lambda T^2+\\mathbf C\\circ T+\\mathbf K=0,\\qquad\n\\mathcal H:=-\\mathbf C-2\\lambda T\\ge m_*I, \\tag{8}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\mathbf C=\\mathbb E\\mathsf C(L),\\qquad\n\\mathbf K=\\mathbb E\\mathsf K(L).\n\\]\nThus \\(R=A-L\\) is orthogonal to Gaussian degrees zero and one.</p>", "proof": "<p>For symmetric \\(Q\\), transform the original data by\n\\[\nC_Q=e^QC_{\\rm in},\\quad D_Q=e^{-Q}D_{\\rm in},\\quad\nU_Q=e^QU_{\\rm in},\\quad V_Q=e^{-Q}V_{\\rm in}.\n\\]</p><p>**Continuity.**\nUnder converging invertible transformations, cone projections converge uniformly on compact sets: projected points are bounded, every subsequential limit minimizes the limiting distance, and uniqueness identifies the limit. Equicontinuity gives compact-uniform convergence. The coercive bound in Lemma 5.1 is locally uniform, so biases converge at every fixed layer.</p><p>Gaussian integration by parts avoids pointwise convergence of projection derivatives:\n\\[\n(\\mathbb EP_z)\\Sigma^{1/2}=\\mathbb EX_zG^T,\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\mathbb EG_iP_z)\\Sigma^{1/2}\n=\\mathbb EX_z(G_iG^T-e_i^T).\n\\]\nThe bounds and tails in Proposition 5 therefore give continuity of\n\\(\\mathbb EA,M_i,\\mathbf C,\\mathbf K\\).</p><p>**Covariance update.**\nPut\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\mathbf C^2-4\\lambda\\mathbf K,\\qquad\nT_{\\rm new}=\\frac{-\\mathbf C-\\sqrt\\Delta}{2\\lambda}.\n\\]\nThe scalar estimates of Proposition 2 imply\n\\[\n\\Delta\\ge m_*^2I,\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\mathbf C+2\\lambda t_+I)^2\n\\le\\Delta\\le\n(\\mathbf C+2\\lambda t_-I)^2,\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathbf C+2\\lambda t_\\pm I&lt;0\\).\nFor the discriminant lower bound use\n\\((\\mathbf C+.37I)^2\\ge0\\); the other inequalities follow by expansion and expectation of the scalar endpoint inequalities.</p><p>The Löwner–Heinz inequality gives\n\\[\n-\\mathbf C-2\\lambda t_+I\n\\le\\sqrt\\Delta\\le\n-\\mathbf C-2\\lambda t_-I.\n\\]\nHence \\(T_{\\rm new}\\) remains in the spectral box, and the discriminant gap makes the update continuous.</p><p>**Uniform inward estimate.**\nLet \\(\\sigma=\\|Q\\|_{\\rm Frob}\\), diagonalize \\(Q\\), write eigenvalues \\(d_j\\), and set\n\\[\nh_j(z)=\\mathbb E(P_z)_{jj},\\qquad\nA_j=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(p-h_j)\\,dz.\n\\]\nInteriority of the original data gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|(X_z)_j|\\le Ca(z)e^{d_j},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E|(Y_z)_j|\\le CB(z)e^{-d_j}.\n\\]\nFor every cutoff \\(J\\ge1\\), Gaussian integration by parts and the Lipschitz bound give\n\\[\nh_j\\le CJ\\mathbb E|(X_z)_j|+Ce^{-cJ^2},\n\\]\n\\[\n1-h_j\\le CJ\\mathbb E|(Y_z)_j|+Ce^{-cJ^2}.\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(\\varepsilon=1/\\sqrt m\\). If \\(d_j\\le0\\), choose \\(J=1+|z|\\) on the negative half-line and use \\(p-h_j\\ge p-1\\) on the positive half-line to obtain \\(A_j\\ge-C\\).\nIf \\(d_j\\le-\\varepsilon\\sigma\\), a large fixed cutoff gives \\(h_j\\le1/4\\) on\n\\([0,e^{\\varepsilon\\sigma/2}]\\), whence\n\\[\nA_j\\ge\\tfrac14e^{\\varepsilon\\sigma/2}-C.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(d_j\\ge0\\), use\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty B\\,dz\\le2s_0.\n\\]\nOn \\([0,e^{2\\sigma}]\\), take \\(J=C_1\\sqrt\\sigma\\), obtaining an integral bound\n\\(C\\sqrt\\sigma e^{-d_j}+o(1)\\) for \\(1-h_j\\).\nFor \\(z\\ge e^{2\\sigma}\\), the transformed interior ball has radius at least\n\\(cae^{-\\sigma}\\), whereas\n\\[\n|\\mathbb EY_z|\\le Ce^\\sigma/a(z).\n\\]\nThus the interior distance is at least \\(cze^{-\\sigma}\\), and the remaining integral is \\(o(1)\\). Hence\n\\[\nA_j\\le C+C\\sqrt\\sigma e^{-d_j}.\n\\]\nIf \\(d_j\\ge\\varepsilon\\sigma\\), choose \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) with \\(\\rho^2/2&lt;\\varepsilon\\). On\n\\([-\\rho\\sqrt\\sigma,0]\\), the bound \\(B\\le s_0/a\\) makes \\(h_j\\ge3/4\\) for large \\(\\sigma\\), so\n\\[\nA_j\\le-c\\sqrt\\sigma+C.\n\\]</p><p>At least one \\(|d_j|\\ge\\varepsilon\\sigma\\). Its contribution to\n\\(\\sum_jd_jA_j=\\operatorname{tr}(Q\\mathbb EA)\\) is negative of order at least\n\\(\\sigma^{3/2}\\), while all remaining contributions are \\(O(\\sigma)\\).\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(Q\\mathbb EA)&lt;0\n\\]\non every sufficiently large \\(Q\\)-sphere, uniformly over \\(T\\).</p><p>**Fixed point.**\nOn a sufficiently large closed Frobenius ball times the spectral box, update \\(T\\) as above and update \\(Q\\) by projecting \\(Q+\\mathbb EA\\) back to the ball.\nBrouwer’s theorem gives a fixed point. A boundary fixed point would make\n\\(\\mathbb EA\\) a nonnegative multiple of \\(Q\\), contradicting the inward estimate. Thus \\(\\mathbb EA=0\\).</p><p>At the fixed point write \\(S=\\sqrt\\Delta\\). Since\n\\(T=(-\\mathbf C-S)/(2\\lambda)\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda T^2+\\mathbf C\\circ T+\\mathbf K\n=\\frac{S^2-\\mathbf C^2}{4\\lambda}+\\mathbf K=0,\n\\]\nwith mixed terms canceling without commutation. Also\n\\(\\mathcal H=S\\ge m_*I\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Entropy inequality — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Entropy inequality — paper: Proposition 4.3.**</p><p>Fix normalized cone data and the Gaussian field of Proposition 6.\nFor the scalar profiles of Proposition 2, define\n\\[\nB_0(z)=a(-z),\\quad q_0(z)=1-p(z),\\quad\n\\Delta B=B-s_0B_0,\\quad \\Delta r=r_1-s_0q_0,\n\\]\n\\[\nh_\\delta=h-s_0p,\\qquad \\Delta\\mu=\\mu-s_0\\gamma.\n\\]\nFor a smooth polynomial-growth test \\(j\\), set\n\\[\n\\delta[j]=-\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n\\{2p\\Delta B\\,j+(p\\Delta r+h_\\delta)(2zj-j')\\}\\,dz. \\tag{10}\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nnt(x,z)=\\tau\\bigl(P_{\\min(x,z)}\\circ(I-P_{\\max(x,z)})\\bigr),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal N=\\iint nt(x,z)\\,dx\\,dz.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\mathcal N\\ge0\\), all these integrals converge, and\n\\[\n\\frac1m\\log(\\chi_C(V)\\chi_D(U))\n\\ge\\tau(\\log\\Sigma-T)+\\tau_TH_v-\\delta[d]+\\frac18\\mathcal N. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nEquivalently, defining\n\\[\n\\mathcal E=\\delta[d]-\\tau_TH_v+\\tau(T-\\log\\Sigma)-\\mathcal N/8,\n\\]\nthe normalized logarithm is at least \\(-\\mathcal E\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The kernel \\(nt\\) is nonnegative because it is the trace of a product of positive semidefinite matrices. Its polynomially weighted integrability follows from the layer tails, splitting \\(x\\le z\\) into the three sign regions.</p><p>Combining Lemmas 7.1 and 7.2, and using\n\\[\n\\gamma v=\\log(2\\pi)-1,\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\frac1m\\log(\\chi_C(V)\\chi_D(U))\n\\ge1+\\tau\\log\\Sigma-\\tau H_v-C_Y+\\mathcal N/8.\n\\]\nThe Gaussian reference identity\n\\[\npB_0+aq_0=\\phi\n\\]\nimplies\n\\[\nd\\Delta\\mu=(p\\Delta B+a\\Delta r)\\,dz=h_\\delta'\\,dz.\n\\]\nIntegration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\tau_\\Sigma H_v=-\\int h_\\delta v'=\\Delta\\mu\\,v.\n\\]\nThe zero mass of \\(\\Delta\\mu\\), together with integration of \\((a\\Delta B)'\\), gives\n\\[\n\\int p\\Delta B=\\int a\\Delta r=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(c_Yq_0=\\phi\\) and \\(c_Y=2a-pv'\\),\n\\[\nC_Y-s_0+\\Delta\\mu\\,v\n=-\\int(p\\Delta r+h_\\delta)v'\\,dz.\n\\]</p><p>For every smooth polynomial-growth test \\(w_0\\),\n\\[\n\\int\\{p\\Delta B(2+N)w_0+(p\\Delta r+h_\\delta)w_0'\\}\\,dz=0. \\tag{12}\n\\]\nIndeed, integration by parts turns this into\n\\[\n\\int\\{2p\\Delta B\\,w_0+(a\\Delta B+h_\\delta)w_0'\\}\\,dz,\n\\]\nwhich vanishes because \\((a\\Delta B+h_\\delta)'=2p\\Delta B\\).\nThe tail estimates justify all boundary limits.</p><p>Apply (12) to \\(g\\), and use (2):\n\\[\n(N+2)g=d,\\qquad 2zd-d'=v'+2g'.\n\\]\nThis proves\n\\[\nC_Y-s_0+\\Delta\\mu\\,v=\\delta[d].\n\\]\nFinally\n\\[\n\\tau H_v=\\Delta\\mu\\,v-\\tau_TH_v,\\qquad s_0=1+\\tau T,\n\\]\nwhich gives (11).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Log-Jacobian gain — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Log-Jacobian gain — paper: Lemma 4.1.**</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 7, put\n\\[\nc_X=\\phi/p,\\quad j_X=z+c_X,\\quad W_X=-c_X'=j_Xc_X,\n\\]\n\\[\nc_Y(z)=c_X(-z),\\qquad W_Y(z)=W_X(-z)=c_Y'(z),\n\\]\n\\[\nJ_X=\\int W_X(z)P_z\\,dz,\\qquad\nJ_Y=\\int W_Y(z)(I-P_z)\\,dz.\n\\]\nThen \\(J_X,J_Y\\) are positive definite almost surely, their log determinants are integrable, and\n\\[\n\\tau(\\log J_X+\\log J_Y)\n\\ge-\\gamma v-\\tau H_v+\\mathcal N/8. \\tag{9}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nJ(z)=\\int_{-\\infty}^zW_X(l)P_l\\,dl,\\qquad\nS_z=J(z)/c_X(z),\n\\]\n\\[\nF_X(z)=\\tau\\log(c_X(z)I+J(z)).\n\\]\nThe matrix decreases with derivative \\(-W_X(I-P_z)\\). Trace differentiation gives\n\\[\nF_X'=-j_X(1-h_1)+\\beta_X,\\qquad h_1=\\tau P_z,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\beta_X=j_X\\tau[S_z(I+S_z)^{-1}(I-P_z)]\\ge0.\n\\]\nCompare with\n\\[\nF_0(z)=\\int\\log c_X(\\min(z,x))\\,d\\gamma(x),\n\\qquad F_0'=-j_X(1-p).\n\\]\nBoth equal \\(\\log c_X(z)+o(1)\\) at negative infinity, so\n\\[\n(F_X-F_0)'=j_X(h_1-p)+\\beta_X.\n\\]\nThe signed term is absolutely integrable. Since \\(F_X\\) decreases and \\(F_0\\) has a finite limit, the integral of \\(\\beta_X\\) is finite and \\(F_X\\) has a finite limit. Monotone convergence for the decreasing matrices then excludes zero eigenvalues of \\(J_X\\) almost surely and proves integrability, with\n\\[\n\\tau\\log J_X\n=\\gamma\\log c_X+\\int j_X(h_1-p)\\,dz+\\int\\beta_X\\,dz.\n\\]\nReflection gives the analogous identity for \\(J_Y\\).</p><p>For \\(l\\le z\\), define\n\\[\nD_{l,z}=c_X(z)^{-1}\\int_l^zW_X(y)P_y\\,dy.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n0\\le D_{l,z}\\le S_z,\\qquad\nD_{l,z}\\le(c_X(l)/c_X(z)-1)I.\n\\]\nOrder preservation of \\(D\\mapsto D(I+D)^{-1}\\) gives\n\\[\nS_z(I+S_z)^{-1}\n\\ge\\frac1{c_X(l)}\\int_l^zW_X(y)P_y\\,dy.\n\\]</p><p>The weight \\(W_X\\) is decreasing. To see this, condition a standard normal \\(g_1\\) on \\(g_1\\le z\\). The variable \\(z-g_1\\) has mean \\(j_X\\), variance \\(j_X'&gt;0\\), and log-concave survival function\n\\(p(z-s)/p(z)\\). Its submultiplicativity gives\n\\[\n\\tfrac12\\mathbb E[(z-g_1)^2\\mid g_1\\le z]\\le j_X^2,\n\\]\nso \\(j_X'\\le j_X^2\\) and \\(W_X'=c_X(j_X'-j_X^2)\\le0\\).</p><p>For \\(z\\ge0\\), choose a positive measure \\(\\omega_z\\) on \\([-z,z]\\) such that\n\\[\n\\int_{[-z,y]}\\frac{d\\omega_z(l)}{c_X(l)}\n=\\frac1{4j_X(z)W_X(y)}.\n\\]\nIts mass is\n\\[\n\\frac1{4j_X(z)^2}+\\frac z{2j_X(z)}\n\\le\\pi/8+1/2&lt;1.\n\\]\nAveraging the matrix bound gives\n\\[\n\\beta_X(z)\\ge\\frac14\\int_{-z}^znt(y,z)\\,dy.\n\\]\nThe reflected estimate covers the complementary half of the ordered region. Together they give surplus at least \\(\\mathcal N/8\\).</p><p>Finally \\(c_Xc_Y=1/(XY)\\) and\n\\(j_X(z)-j_X(-z)=v'(z)\\); the scalar and linear terms become\n\\(-\\gamma v-\\tau H_v\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Entropy from projection maps — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Entropy from projection maps — paper: Lemma 4.2.**</p><p>With the notation of Lemma 7.1 and\n\\[\nC_Y=\\int W_YB\\,dz=\\int c_Yr_1\\,dz,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\frac1m\\log(\\chi_C(V)\\chi_D(U))\n\\ge\\log(2\\pi e)+\\tau\\log\\Sigma\n+\\tau(\\log J_X+\\log J_Y)-1-C_Y.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(Z'\\) be independent with the same law as \\(Z\\), fix \\(0&lt;\\zeta&lt;1\\), and put\n\\[\n\\widetilde Z=\\zeta Z+\\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}Z'.\n\\]\nFor integers \\(j\\ge1\\), define\n\\[\nT_{X,j}(G)=\\int_{-j}^jW_X(z)\\mathbb E_{Z'}X_z(\\widetilde Z)\\,dz,\n\\]\nand analogously \\(T_{Y,j}\\). Their Jacobians are\n\\[\nJ_{X,j}\\zeta\\Sigma^{1/2},\\qquad\n-J_{Y,j}\\zeta\\Sigma^{1/2},\n\\]\nwhere the factors integrate the conditionally smoothed \\(P_z\\) and \\(I-P_z\\).</p><p>The factors are positive definite, since the conditional Gaussian sees an open set where the corresponding derivative is \\(I\\). As functions of \\(Z\\), the maps have symmetric positive or negative definite derivatives. Integration on line segments gives injectivity. Their images are open subsets of the cone interiors, and the inverse function theorem gives smooth inverses.</p><p>For fixed \\(j,\\zeta\\), the factors are bounded below by\n\\(ce^{-C(1+|Z|)^2}I\\), using a fixed interior ball and a compact layer subinterval; their upper bounds are deterministic. Thus the log determinants are integrable, and the maps have finite first moments.</p><p>With \\(\\rho\\) the standard Gaussian density, change of variables over the image and Jensen give\n\\[\n\\log\\chi_C(V)\\ge\n\\frac m2\\log(2\\pi e)\n+\\mathbb E\\log|\\det D_GT_{X,j}|\n-\\mathbb E[V\\cdot T_{X,j}].\n\\]\nApply the same argument to \\(D\\).\nAs \\(j\\to\\infty\\), matrix monotone convergence and conditional concavity of log determinant give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\log\\det\\mathbb E_{Z'}J_X(\\widetilde Z)\n\\ge\\mathbb E\\log\\det J_X(Z),\n\\]\nand similarly for \\(Y\\), with Lemma 7.1 supplying integrability.</p><p>The costs converge to\n\\[\n\\int W_Xa=\\int c_Xp=\\int\\phi=1,\\qquad\n\\int W_YB=\\int c_Yr_1=C_Y.\n\\]\nThe Jacobian right factors contribute\n\\(2\\log\\zeta+\\tau\\log\\Sigma\\). Let \\(\\zeta\\uparrow1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Quadratic decomposition of the entropy term — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Quadratic decomposition of the entropy term — paper: Proposition 5.7.**</p><p>Fix the normalized cone field of Proposition 6, with the scalar profiles of Proposition 2, \\(H,H_0\\) of Proposition 3, and \\(\\delta,\\Delta\\mu\\) of Proposition 7.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nN_G=G\\cdot\\nabla_G-\\Delta_G,\\qquad K_G=(1+N_G)^{-1},\n\\]\nacting entrywise. For a symmetric field \\(E\\), define the form norm\n\\[\n\\|E\\|_{1,\\Sigma}^2\n=\\tau_\\Sigma E^2+\\sum_i\\tau_\\Sigma(\\partial_{G_i}E)^2.\n\\]\nFor a scalar test \\(l\\), let \\(h_l\\) be its Gaussian-centered antiderivative, and put\n\\[\nf_l=(1+N)h_l,\\quad S_l=f_l'=(2+N)l,\\quad\n\\psi_l'=(N-1)h_l.\n\\]\nChoose the additive constants in \\(\\psi_l\\) linearly in \\(l\\). Define\n\\[\nd_l^*=K_GH_{f_l}-h_l(L),\\quad\nD_\\Sigma(l)^2=\\|d_l^*\\|_{1,\\Sigma}^2,\\quad\n\\sigma_\\Sigma(l)=2\\langle R,d_l^*\\rangle_\\Sigma,\n\\]\n\\[\nJ_\\Sigma(l)=\\tau_\\Sigma\\!\\left(Lh_{l^2}(L)\\right)\n-\\sum_i\\tau_\\Sigma(\\partial_{G_i}h_l(L))^2.\n\\]\nFor vector-valued \\(l\\), sum the quantities over components.</p><p>For deterministic symmetric \\(\\Lambda\\), set\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\Lambda j=\\tau_\\Lambda(j(L)-(\\gamma j)I).\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\n\\Theta_z(B)=1_{(-\\infty,z]}(B),\\qquad E_z^B=P_z-\\Theta_z(B).\n\\]\nDefine the positive layer measure\n\\[\n\\int F(z,x)\\,d\\beta^B(z,x)\n=\\int dz\\,\\tau\\!\\left[E_z^B(B-zI)F(z,B)\\right].\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\beta=\\beta^L\\), \\(\\mathcal B=\\beta(\\mathbb R^2)\\).\nFor a two-variable profile \\(\\Phi\\), set\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_\\Lambda[\\Phi]\n=\\int dz\\left\\langle E_z^L,\n x\\mapsto\\int_z^x\\Phi(z,y)\\,dy\\bigg|_{x=L}\\right\\rangle_\\Lambda.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_I[\\Phi]=\\int\\bar\\Phi(z,x)\\,d\\beta(z,x),\n\\]\nwhere the bar is the segment average in the second variable.</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\delta[d]-\\tau_TH_v\\le{}&amp;\n\\Delta_p+\\sigma_\\Sigma(F)-D_\\Sigma(u)^2-J_\\Sigma(u)\n-\\langle R,v'(L)\\rangle_T\\\\\n&amp;-\\mathcal L_I[H]-\\mathcal L_T[H+v''(x)], \\qquad(18)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\Delta_p=\\Delta_I\\psi_d+\\Delta_T(\\psi_d-v).\n\\]\nAll displayed fields, forms, and integrals are finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the first identity of Lemma 8.6 for \\(F=c+2kq\\), and subtract the quadratic identity for each component of \\(u\\), using \\(d=F-|u|^2\\).\nThe combined layer profile is\n\\[\n2F(x)+S_F(z)-2S_u(z)\\cdot u(x)=H(z,x).\n\\]\nThe subtracted contact integral is\n\\[\n\\iint ct(x,z)\\{S_F(x)+S_F(z)-S_u(x)\\cdot S_u(z)\\}\\,dx\\,dz.\n\\]\nIt is nonnegative by \\(ct\\ge0\\) and Proposition 3, so it may be discarded.</p><p>Finally, subtract \\(\\tau_TH_v\\) using Lemma 8.5:\nits remainder term is \\(-\\langle R,v'(L)\\rangle_T\\), and its layer term is\n\\(-e_T(\\iota,v')=-\\mathcal L_T[v''(x)]\\).\nSplitting \\(\\Sigma=I+T\\) gives (18), with\n\\(\\Delta_p=\\Delta_I\\psi_d+\\Delta_T(\\psi_d-v)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Gaussian covariance formula — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Gaussian covariance formula — paper: Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>For centered Gaussian Sobolev vector fields\n\\(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal Y\\in W^{1,2}(\\mathbb R^m;\\mathbb R^m)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(\\mathcal X\\cdot\\mathcal Y)\n=\\mathbb E\\sum_i(\\partial_{G_i}\\mathcal X)\\cdot\n K_G(\\partial_{G_i}\\mathcal Y).\n\\]\nFor every square-integrable symmetric matrix field \\(E\\), \\(K_GE\\) belongs to the form domain of \\(1+N_G\\), also with deterministic positive weight \\(\\Sigma\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In the orthonormal multivariate Hermite basis, \\(N_G\\) has eigenvalue \\(|a|\\), while differentiation in coordinate \\(i\\) lowers the index with factor \\(\\sqrt{a_i}\\).\nFor each nonconstant mode,\n\\[\n\\sum_{i:a_i&gt;0}\\frac{a_i}{1+|a|-1}=1.\n\\]\nThis proves the covariance identity for finite Hermite sums and then by Sobolev convergence.</p><p>If \\(E_a\\) is a matrix Hermite coefficient, its contribution to the form norm of \\(K_GE\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\langle E_a,E_a\\rangle_\\Sigma}{1+|a|},\n\\]\nwhich is summable. The deterministic weight acts only on matrix coefficients and preserves Hermite degrees.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Covariance of layer integrals — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Covariance of layer integrals — paper: Lemma 5.2.**</p><p>Define\n\\[\nQ[f,j]=\\iint f(x)j(z)p(\\min(x,z))r_1(\\max(x,z))\\,dx\\,dz,\n\\]\n\\[\nct(x,z)=m^{-1}\\mathbb E[X_{\\min(x,z)}\\cdot Y_{\\max(x,z)}],\n\\]\n\\[\nCt(f,j)=\\iint ct(x,z)f'(x)j'(z)\\,dx\\,dz.\n\\]\nThe density of \\(Q\\) is nonnegative and has both marginals \\(\\mu\\); \\(ct\\ge0\\).\nFor smooth polynomial-growth tests with \\(\\gamma f=\\gamma j=0\\),\n\\[\n\\langle H_f,K_GH_j\\rangle_\\Sigma=Q[f,j]+Ct(f,j). \\tag{13}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The marginals follow by integrating the kernel:\n\\[\na(z)r_1(z)+p(z)B(z)=h'(z).\n\\]\nPositive duality gives \\(ct\\ge0\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 8.1 to the Sobolev vector fields \\(W_f,W_j\\) from Proposition 5.\nFor \\(x\\le z\\),\n\\[\nm^{-1}\\mathbb E[(X_x-\\mathbb EX_x)\\cdot(X_z-\\mathbb EX_z)]\n=h(x)-a(x)B(z)+ct(x,z).\n\\]\nThe remaining covariance kernel of \\(W_f,W_j\\), apart from\n\\(ct-a(\\min)B(\\max)\\), is\n\\[\ns_0p(x)p(z)-p(x)h(z)-h(x)p(z)+h(\\min(x,z)).\n\\]\nIt equals\n\\[\n\\int(p(x)-1_{\\{y\\le x\\}})(p(z)-1_{\\{y\\le z\\}})\\,d\\mu(y).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\int(p(z)-1_{\\{y\\le z\\}})f'(z)\\,dz=f(y)\n\\]\nfor centered \\(f\\), its contribution is \\(\\mu[fj]\\).</p><p>The mixed distributional derivative of \\(a(\\min(x,z))B(\\max(x,z))\\) is\n\\[\nd\\mu(x)\\delta_x(dz)\n-p(\\min(x,z))r_1(\\max(x,z))\\,dx\\,dz.\n\\]\nDouble integration by parts therefore gives \\(\\mu[fj]-Q[f,j]\\), proving (13).\nPolynomially weighted integrability follows by the three sign-region split and the layer tails, so cutoff approximation justifies noncompact tests.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Reference inversion and variation — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Reference inversion and variation — paper: Lemma 5.3.**</p><p>Let \\(Q_0\\) be \\(Q\\) with \\(r_1\\) replaced by \\(q_0=1-p\\). For smooth polynomial-growth functions \\(b_1,b_2\\), put\n\\[\nf=(1+N)b_1,\\qquad j=(1+N)b_2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nQ_0[f,j]=\\gamma[fb_2],\n\\]\n\\[\n(Q-s_0Q_0)[f,j]\n=\\Delta\\mu\\,w+\\delta[b_1'b_2'],\n\\qquad w'=f'b_2+j'b_1. \\tag{14}\n\\]\nThe additive constant in \\(w\\) is immaterial.</p>", "proof": "<p>Direct differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\int_{-\\infty}^zpf=ab_1-pb_1',\\qquad\n\\int_z^\\infty q_0f=B_0b_1+q_0b_1',\n\\]\nand similarly for \\(j\\). Since \\(q_0a+pB_0=\\phi\\), substitution gives\n\\(Q_0[f,j]=\\gamma[jb_1]=\\gamma[fb_2]\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nH_1=fb_2+jb_1,\\qquad J_1=fb_2'+jb_1',\\qquad\nw'=H_1'-J_1.\n\\]\nSplitting into ordered half-planes gives\n\\[\n(Q-s_0Q_0)[f,j]=\\int\\Delta r(aH_1-pJ_1).\n\\]\nUse \\(\\Delta r=-(\\Delta B)'\\),\n\\(\\Delta\\mu\\,w=-\\int h_\\delta w'\\), and\n\\((a\\Delta B+h_\\delta)'=2p\\Delta B\\).\nAfter integration by parts, subtraction of \\(\\Delta\\mu\\,w\\) leaves\n\\[\n-\\int\\{p\\Delta B\\,H_1+(p\\Delta r+h_\\delta)J_1\\}.\n\\]\nThe product rule gives\n\\[\nH_1=(2+N)(b_1b_2)+2b_1'b_2',\n\\]\n\\[\nJ_1=(b_1b_2)'+2zb_1'b_2'-(b_1'b_2')'.\n\\]\nEquation (12) cancels the product terms, leaving\n\\(\\delta[b_1'b_2']\\). All boundary terms vanish by the weighted tail bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Quadratic threshold defect — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Quadratic threshold defect — paper: Lemma 5.4.**</p><p>For a symmetric random matrix \\(B\\) with all finite moments, define\n\\[\nM_f^B=H_f-f(B)+(\\gamma f)I=-\\int E_z^Bf'(z)\\,dz,\n\\]\n\\[\ne_\\Lambda^B(f,j)\n=\\int f'(z)\\langle E_z^B,j(B)-j(z)I\\rangle_\\Lambda\\,dz,\n\\]\n\\[\nNt_\\Lambda(f,j)\n=\\iint\\tau_\\Lambda(P_{\\min(x,z)}\\circ(I-P_{\\max(x,z)}))\n                         f'(x)j'(z)\\,dx\\,dz.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\langle M_f^B,M_f^B\\rangle_\\Lambda\n=2e_\\Lambda^B(f,f)-Nt_\\Lambda(f,f). \\tag{15}\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n2e_\\Lambda^L(\\iota,\\iota)=\\tau_\\Lambda R^2+\\mathcal N_\\Lambda,\\qquad\n2e_\\Lambda^A(\\iota,\\iota)=\\mathcal N_\\Lambda,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal N_\\Lambda=Nt_\\Lambda(\\iota,\\iota)\\).\nAlso \\(\\beta^B\\) is positive and has mass \\(e_I^B(\\iota,\\iota)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\Theta_z=\\Theta_z(B)\\). For \\(x&lt;z\\),\n\\(\\Theta_x\\Theta_z=\\Theta_x\\), and expansion gives\n\\[\nE_x^B\\circ E_z^B+P_x\\circ(I-P_z)\n=P_x\\circ(I-\\Theta_z)+(I-P_z)\\circ\\Theta_x.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\(f'(x)f'(z)\\), integrate over both orders, and use\n\\[\nf(B)-f(y)I=\\int(1_{\\{y\\le l\\}}I-\\Theta_l)f'(l)\\,dl.\n\\]\nThe result is (15). For \\(\\iota\\), \\(M_\\iota^L=R\\), \\(M_\\iota^A=0\\).</p><p>In a spectral basis of \\(B\\), each summand defining \\(\\beta^B\\) is\n\\[\n(E_z^B)_{ii}(x_i-z)\\ge0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(0\\le P_z\\le I\\). This also identifies its mass.\nThe moment and layer bounds justify all integrations without nesting or commutation assumptions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Layer-to-spectral comparison — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Layer-to-spectral comparison — paper: Lemma 5.5.**</p><p>Suppress the superscript \\(L\\) in \\(e_\\Lambda^L\\). For smooth polynomial-growth tests,\n\\[\n\\tau_\\Lambda H_j\n=\\Delta_\\Lambda j+\\langle R,j'(L)\\rangle_\\Lambda\n+e_\\Lambda(\\iota,j'),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\tau_\\Sigma H_j=\\Delta\\mu\\,j,\n\\]\nand, if \\(\\gamma f=0\\),\n\\[\n\\langle H_f,j(L)\\rangle_\\Sigma\n=\\mu[fj]+(\\Delta_\\Sigma-\\Delta\\mu)w-e_\\Sigma(f,j),\n\\qquad w'=fj'. \\tag{16}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(R=-\\int E_z^L\\,dz\\),\n\\[\n\\langle R,j'(L)\\rangle_\\Lambda+e_\\Lambda(\\iota,j')\n=-\\int j'(z)\\tau_\\Lambda E_z^L\\,dz,\n\\]\nwhich proves the first formula.\nIntegration by parts in\n\\[\n\\tau_\\Sigma H_j=\\int(s_0p-h)j'\n\\]\ngives the second.\nFor the third, subtract \\(f(L)\\) from \\(H_f\\), write\n\\(j(L)=(j(L)-j(z)I)+j(z)I\\), and use\n\\(f'j=(fj-w)'\\) together with the second formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Linear and quadratic profile identities — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Linear and quadratic profile identities — paper: Lemma 5.6.**</p><p>For every scalar test \\(l\\) and the definitions in Proposition 8,\n\\[\n\\delta[l]\n=\\Delta_\\Sigma\\psi_l+\\sigma_\\Sigma(l)\n-2e_\\Sigma(\\iota,h_l)-e_\\Sigma(f_l,\\iota)-2Ct(\\iota,f_l),\n\\]\n\\[\nD_\\Sigma(l)^2+J_\\Sigma(l)\n=\\delta[l^2]-\\Delta_\\Sigma\\psi_{l^2}\n+2e_\\Sigma(f_l,h_l)+Ct(f_l,f_l). \\tag{17}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>All form quantities are finite. In a spectral basis of \\(L\\),\n\\[\n(\\partial_{G_i}h_l(L))_{ab}\n=h_l^{[1]}(x_a,x_b)(M_i)_{ab},\n\\]\nwhere the divided difference equals\n\\((h_l(x)-h_l(y))/(x-y)\\), or \\(h_l'(x)\\) at coincidence.\nThe formula follows for polynomials and then by \\(C^1\\) approximation on compact spectral intervals. Polynomial growth gives square-integrable derivatives; Lemma 8.1 handles \\(K_GH_{f_l}\\).</p><p>For the linear identity, evaluate\n\\(\\mu[\\iota f_l]-2Q[\\iota,f_l]\\) twice.\nSince \\((1+N)^{-1}\\iota=\\iota/2\\), Lemma 8.3 gives\n\\[\n\\mu[\\iota f_l]-2Q[\\iota,f_l]=\\Delta\\mu\\,\\psi_l-\\delta[l].\n\\]\nLemmas 8.2 and 8.5, \\(H_\\iota=L+R\\), and \\(K_GL=L/2\\), give instead\n\\[\n-(\\Delta_\\Sigma-\\Delta\\mu)v_0+e_\\Sigma(f_l,\\iota)\n-2\\langle R,K_GH_{f_l}\\rangle_\\Sigma+2Ct(\\iota,f_l),\n\\qquad v_0'=f_l.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 8.5 to \\(\\psi_l-v_0\\), whose derivative is \\(-2h_l\\).\nThe \\(R\\)-pairings combine to \\(\\sigma_\\Sigma(l)\\), giving the first identity.</p><p>For the quadratic identity, write \\(b_1=h_l\\), \\(f=f_l\\), and expand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_\\Sigma(l)^2={}&amp;\n\\langle H_f,K_GH_f\\rangle_\\Sigma\n-2\\langle H_f,b_1(L)\\rangle_\\Sigma+\\tau_\\Sigma b_1(L)^2\\\\\n&amp;+\\sum_i\\tau_\\Sigma(\\partial_{G_i}b_1(L))^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAdding \\(J_\\Sigma(l)\\) cancels the Dirichlet term.\nInsert Lemmas 8.2, 8.3, and 8.5.\nThe reference terms cancel because\n\\[\n\\gamma[xh_{l^2}]=\\gamma[l^2]=\\gamma[(f-b_1)b_1].\n\\]\nThe \\(\\Delta\\mu\\) primitive has zero derivative. The remaining\n\\(\\Delta_\\Sigma\\) primitive is\n\\[\nb_1^2+xh_{l^2}-2\\int^x fb_1',\n\\]\nwhose derivative is\n\\[\nh_{l^2}-xl^2+2ll'=-(N-1)h_{l^2}=-\\psi_{l^2}'.\n\\]\nThis proves the second identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Control of the layer errors — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Control of the layer errors — paper: Section 6, culminating in equation (23).**</p><p>Fix the normalized field of Proposition 6 and the definitions in Propositions 7–8. Let\n\\[\na_0=b+\\eta+(b+\\eta-\\kappa)t_-,\n\\]\n\\[\nP_0(z,x)=2a_0+2b_mS_q(z)S_q(x)+2e_0(2t_r)^2.\n\\]\nFor a two-variable function, let the bar denote its uniform average in the second variable between \\(z\\) and \\(x\\), including its value at coincidence. Define\n\\[\ng_z(x)=\\overline{H_0}(z,x),\n\\]\n\\[\nW(z,x)=\\overline{H-P_0}(z,x)+t_cg_z(x)\n-\\frac{t_r}{2}(h_s+g_z(x)^2/h_s),\n\\]\n\\[\nW_0(z,x)=\\overline{H_0^2}(z,x)/\\alpha.\n\\]\nThen \\(W&gt;W_0\\ge0\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathcal E\\le\n\\Delta_p-J_\\Sigma(u)+.80\\|[L,T]\\|_2^2+1.8\\tau T^2\n-\\int(W-W_0)\\,d\\beta. \\tag{23}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Averaging Proposition 3 and using\n\\((\\overline{H_0})^2\\le\\overline{H_0^2}\\) gives \\(W&gt;W_0\\ge0\\).</p><p>In Proposition 8, use \\(H+v''=2\\kappa+H_0\\) and Lemma 8.4:\n\\[\n-2\\kappa\\mathcal L_T[1]=-\\kappa\\tau_TR^2-\\kappa\\mathcal N_T.\n\\]\nCombining with Lemma 9.1,\n\\[\n(b+\\eta)\\tau_\\Sigma R^2-\\kappa\\tau_TR^2\n\\le2a_0\\mathcal B-(b+\\eta)\\mathcal N.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\tau R^2=2\\mathcal B-\\mathcal N\\), \\(\\mathcal N\\ge0\\), and\n\\(b+\\eta-\\kappa&lt;0\\).</p><p>Including \\(-\\mathcal N/8\\) in \\(\\mathcal E\\), Lemma 9.3 leaves\n\\[\n-\\Lambda_0\\mathcal N+\n\\kappa\\sqrt{c_g\\mathcal N/2}\\,\\|[A,T]\\|_2,\n\\qquad\n\\Lambda_0=b+\\eta+\\kappa t_-+\\tfrac18=.72348.\n\\]\nCompleting the square bounds this by\n\\[\n\\frac{\\kappa^2c_g}{8\\Lambda_0}\\|[A,T]\\|_2^2\n\\le e_0\\|[A,T]\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThe strict numerical comparison follows from \\(\\log2&lt;.694\\).</p><p>Hermite orthogonality and the spectral range of \\(T\\) imply\n\\[\ne_0\\|[A,T]\\|_2^2\n\\le e_0\\|[L,T]\\|_2^2+2e_0(2t_r)^2\\mathcal B.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal E\\le{}&amp;\n\\Delta_p-J_\\Sigma(u)+e_0\\|[L,T]\\|_2^2\n-\\eta\\tau_\\Sigma R_o^2-\\langle R,v'(L)\\rangle_T\\\\\n&amp;+\\tau(T-\\log\\Sigma)\n-\\mathcal L_I[H-P_0]-\\mathcal L_T[H_0]. \\qquad(21)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nApply Lemma 9.4.</p><p>Since \\(v'\\) and \\(L\\) are odd and \\(R_o\\) is orthogonal to degree one,\n\\[\n\\langle R,v'(L)\\rangle_T\n=\\langle R_o,v'(L)-L\\rangle_T.\n\\]\nProposition 2 gives\n\\[\n|\\langle R_o,v'(L)-L\\rangle_T|\n\\le.319(\\tau R_o^2)^{1/2}(\\tau T^2)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nComplete the square, using \\(\\Sigma\\ge(1+t_-)I\\), and use\n\\[\nt-\\log(1+t)\\le t^2/[2(1+t_-)].\n\\]\nThe resulting coefficient is\n\\[\n\\frac{.319^2}{4\\eta(1+t_-)}+\\frac1{2(1+t_-)}\n=\\frac{4417}{2608}&lt;1.8.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\ne_0+\\frac{\\alpha(\\log2+.25)}4&lt;.78936&lt;.80.\n\\]\nThese substitutions give (23).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Gaussian remainder — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Gaussian remainder — paper: Lemma 6.1.**</p><p>Write\n\\[\nR_e(G)=\\frac{R(G)+R(-G)}2,\\qquad\nR_o(G)=\\frac{R(G)-R(-G)}2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sigma_\\Sigma(F)-D_\\Sigma(u)^2\n\\le(b+\\eta)\\tau_\\Sigma R^2-\\eta\\tau_\\Sigma R_o^2\n+b_m\\|M_{f_q}^L\\|_2^2, \\tag{19}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|M_{f_q}^L\\|_2^2\\le2\\mathcal L_I[S_q(z)S_q(x)].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The normalization gives degrees at least \\(2\\) for \\(R_e\\) and at least \\(3\\) for \\(R_o\\).\nFor the constant profile \\(1\\),\n\\[\nd_1^*=2K_GR.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\sigma_\\Sigma(F)=4c\\langle R,K_GR\\rangle_\\Sigma\n+4k\\langle R,d_q^*\\rangle_\\Sigma.\n\\]\nDiscarding the nonnegative circle contribution to \\(D_\\Sigma(u)^2\\), complete the square on odd degrees:\n\\[\n4k\\langle R_o,(d_q^*)_o\\rangle_\\Sigma\n-\\|(d_q^*)_o\\|_{1,\\Sigma}^2\n\\le4k^2\\langle R_o,K_GR_o\\rangle_\\Sigma.\n\\]\nSince \\(c+k^2=b\\) and \\(K_G\\le1/4\\) there, the odd contribution is at most \\(b\\tau_\\Sigma R_o^2\\).</p><p>Because \\(q\\) is even, \\(h_q,f_q\\) are odd, and\n\\[\n(d_q^*)_e=K_G(M_{f_q}^L)_e.\n\\]\nPut \\(\\widehat b=b+\\eta\\) and\n\\[\nb_m'=\\frac{b_m}{1+t_+}\n=\\frac{b-3\\eta}{3(3\\widehat b-4c)}\n=\\frac{134}{309}.\n\\]\nFor \\(0\\le\\rho\\le1/3\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}4c\\rho&amp;2k\\rho\\\\2k\\rho&amp;-\\rho\\end{pmatrix}\n\\le\n\\begin{pmatrix}\\widehat b&amp;0\\\\0&amp;b_m'\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe first diagonal gap is at least \\(103/750\\), and the determinant is\n\\[\n(1-3\\rho)(\\widehat b\\,b_m'+4b\\rho/3)\\ge0.\n\\]\nSumming the even Hermite degrees and using \\(\\Sigma\\le(1+t_+)I\\) proves (19).</p><p>Lemma 8.4 gives\n\\[\n\\|M_{f_q}^L\\|_2^2=2e(f_q,f_q)-Nt(f_q,f_q).\n\\]\nSince \\(nt\\ge0\\) and \\(f_q'=S_q&gt;0\\), the final term is nonnegative, yielding the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Positive blocks and weighted diagonal bounds — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Positive blocks and weighted diagonal bounds — paper: Lemma 6.2.**</p><p>Let \\(B\\) be real symmetric, \\(0\\le P\\le I\\), and let \\(z\\) be outside the spectrum of \\(B\\). In a spectral basis, write\n\\[\nE=P-1_{(-\\infty,z]}(B),\\quad\na_i=|x_i-z|,\\quad \\varepsilon_i=\\operatorname{sgn}(x_i-z).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n(E^2)_{ii}\\le\\varepsilon_iE_{ii}.\n\\]\nThe matrix\n\\[\nS_{ij}=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\varepsilon_i\\sqrt{a_ia_j}\\,E_{ij},&amp;\\varepsilon_i=\\varepsilon_j,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\varepsilon_i\\ne\\varepsilon_j\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nis positive semidefinite, with\n\\(\\operatorname{tr}S=\\sum_i a_i\\varepsilon_iE_{ii}\\).\nFor \\(w_i\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{i,j}\\frac{a_iw_i+a_jw_j}{2}|E_{ij}|^2\n\\le\\sum_i a_iw_i\\varepsilon_iE_{ii}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(Q=1_{(-\\infty,z]}(B)\\), \\(P^2\\le P\\) gives\n\\[\n(E^2)_{ii}=(P^2)_{ii}-2Q_{ii}P_{ii}+Q_{ii}\n\\le\\varepsilon_iE_{ii}.\n\\]\nThe positive-side compression of \\(E\\) is that of \\(P\\); the negative-side compression of \\(-E\\) is that of \\(I-P\\). Both are positive semidefinite. Diagonal congruence by \\(\\sqrt{a_i}\\) proves \\(S\\ge0\\).\nFinally symmetry gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i,j}\\frac{a_iw_i+a_jw_j}{2}|E_{ij}|^2\n=\\sum_i a_iw_i(E^2)_{ii},\n\\]\nand the first inequality completes the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Weighted constant layer — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Weighted constant layer — paper: Lemma 6.3.**</p><p>Let \\(c_g=2\\log2-\\frac13\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_T=2e_T^A(\\iota,\\iota)\n\\ge t_-\\mathcal N-\\sqrt{c_g\\mathcal N/2}\\,\\|[A,T]\\|_2. \\tag{20}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.4 gives \\(\\mathcal N_T=2e_T^A(\\iota,\\iota)\\),\n\\(\\mathcal N=2\\mathcal B^A\\).\nIn a spectral basis of \\(A\\), the kernel in \\(2e_T^A\\) is \\(x_i+x_j-2z\\).\nCompare it with \\(2\\varepsilon_i\\sqrt{a_ia_j}\\) on same-sign blocks and \\(0\\) otherwise. Its pairing is \\(2\\operatorname{tr}(TS_z)\\), at least \\(2t_-\\operatorname{tr}S_z\\) by Lemma 9.2.</p><p>Let \\(k_z(x_i,x_j)\\) be the remaining kernel and\n\\(\\omega_z=(a_i+a_j)/2\\). Then\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{k_z(x_i,x_j)^2}{\\omega_z}\\,dz\n=c_g(x_i-x_j)^2.\n\\]\nFor endpoints \\(0,1\\), the middle interval contributes \\(2/3\\); the two exterior intervals contribute\n\\[\n4\\int_0^\\infty\n\\frac{(\\sqrt{1+t}-\\sqrt t)^4}{1+2t}\\,dt\n=2\\log2-1.\n\\]\nUse \\(u=(\\sqrt{1+t}-\\sqrt t)^2\\) to evaluate the latter integral.</p><p>Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz and Lemma 9.2 bound the remaining pairing by\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\mathcal B^A}\\,\\sqrt{c_g}\\,\\|[A,T]\\|_2.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathcal B^A=\\mathcal N/2\\), (20) follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Variable weighted layer — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Variable weighted layer — paper: Lemma 6.4.**</p><p>With \\(W,W_0\\) from Proposition 9,\n\\[\n-\\mathcal L_I[H-P_0]-\\mathcal L_T[H_0]\n\\le\\frac{\\alpha(\\log2+.25)}4\\|[L,T]\\|_2^2\n-\\int(W-W_0)\\,d\\beta. \\tag{22}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>In a spectral basis of \\(L\\), put \\(g_i=g_z(x_i)\\). The kernel paired with\n\\(T_{ji}(E_z^L)_{ij}\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{(x_i-z)g_i+(x_j-z)g_j}{2}.\n\\]\nCompare it with\n\\(\\varepsilon_i\\sqrt{a_ia_j}(g_i+g_j)/2\\) on same-sign blocks and zero otherwise. The comparison pairing is\n\\(\\operatorname{tr}(S_z(T\\circ g_z(L)))\\).</p><p>For any symmetric \\(V_0\\),\n\\[\nT\\circ V_0\\ge t_cV_0-\\frac{t_r}{2}(h_sI+V_0^2/h_s),\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\|T-t_cI\\|\\le t_r\\) and\n\\[\n|\\langle v,(T-t_cI)V_0v\\rangle|\n\\le\\frac{t_r}{2}(h_s|v|^2+h_s^{-1}|V_0v|^2).\n\\]\nPair with \\(S_z\\ge0\\) and integrate. Together with \\(\\mathcal L_I[H-P_0]\\), this contributes \\(-\\int W\\,d\\beta\\).</p><p>For the remainder kernel \\(k_z(x_i,x_j)\\), use\n\\[\nD_z=\\frac{a_iW_0(z,x_i)+a_jW_0(z,x_j)}2.\n\\]\nLemma 9.2 bounds the integrated weighted square of \\(E_z^L\\) by\n\\(\\int W_0\\,d\\beta\\).\nThe scalar kernel bound is\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{k_z(x_i,x_j)^2}{D_z}\\,dz\n\\le\\alpha(\\log2+.25)(x_i-x_j)^2.\n\\]\nAt zero weights the numerator also vanishes.</p><p>For endpoints a distance \\(\\ell\\) apart, the middle interval contributes at most\n\\(\\alpha\\ell^2/2\\), by Cauchy–Schwarz on the integrals of \\(H_0\\).\nOutside the interval, with distances \\(a=\\ell(1+t)\\), \\(b=\\ell t\\), one has\n\\[\nk_z^2=\\frac{(\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b)^2}{4}\n      (\\sqrt a\\,g_a-\\sqrt b\\,g_b)^2.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz with multiplicities \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) yields the exterior contribution\n\\[\n\\alpha\\ell^2\\int_0^\\infty(\\sqrt{1+t}-\\sqrt t)^2\n\\left[\\frac1{1+t}+\\frac12\\left(1-\\sqrt{\\frac t{1+t}}\\right)^2\\right]dt\n=\\alpha\\ell^2(\\log2-\\tfrac14).\n\\]\nThis proves the scalar bound.</p><p>Writing \\(V_*= \\int W_0\\,d\\beta\\), the remainder pairing is at most\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\alpha(\\log2+.25)V_*}\\,\\|[L,T]\\|_2.\n\\]\nNow split \\(W=(W-W_0)+W_0\\), and complete the square in \\(\\sqrt{V_*}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Linear-field estimate and its equality consequences — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Linear-field estimate and its equality consequences — paper: Proposition 7.2.**</p><p>Let \\(L=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^mG_\\ell M_\\ell\\), where \\(M_\\ell\\) are deterministic real symmetric matrices. Let \\(T\\) satisfy the spectral box and normalization (8), with\n\\[\n\\mathbf C=\\mathbb E\\mathsf C(L),\\qquad\n\\mathbf K=\\mathbb E\\mathsf K(L).\n\\]\nUse the scalar profiles of Propositions 2–4 and the functionals\n\\(\\Delta_p,J_\\Sigma\\) of Proposition 8.</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\Delta_p-J_\\Sigma(u)+.80\\|[L,T]\\|_2^2\n\\le-1.94\\tau T^2.\n\\]\nConsequently, for the cone field,\n\\[\n\\mathcal E\\le-.14\\tau T^2-\\int(W-W_0)\\,d\\beta\\le0.\n\\]</p><p>At each Gaussian input choose a spectral basis of \\(L\\), with eigenvalues \\(x_i\\), and define the positive symmetric measure\n\\[\n\\nu[\\Phi]=\\frac1m\\mathbb E\n \\sum_{\\ell,i,j}|(M_\\ell)_{ij}|^2\\Phi(x_i,x_j).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\mathcal E=0\\), then\n\\[\nT=0,\\qquad \\nu\\text{ is supported on }\\{x=y\\},\\qquad \\beta=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>**Scalar functional.**\nThe identities of Proposition 2 imply\n\\[\n\\psi_d''=(N+2)\\mathsf K,\\qquad\n(\\psi_d-v)''=(N+2)(\\mathsf C-a_Rg'').\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nA_{\\mathsf K}(x)=\\int_0^x(x-y)\\mathsf K(y)\\,dy,\\qquad\nA_{\\mathsf C}(x)=\\int_0^x(x-y)\\mathsf C(y)\\,dy.\n\\]\nEvenness shows\n\\[\n\\psi_d=NA_{\\mathsf K}+\\text{constant},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\psi_d-v=NA_{\\mathsf C}-a_RNg+\\text{constant}.\n\\]\nSince \\(Ng=\\mathsf K\\) and Gaussian means of \\(N\\)-images vanish, Lemma 10.1 gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_p={}&amp;\\nu[e_{\\mathsf K}]\n+\\tau[(S-I)(\\mathbf K+T\\circ\\mathbf C)]\n-a_R\\tau(T\\mathbf K)+\\tau_T\\mathcal R(\\mathsf C)\\\\\n={}&amp;\\nu[e_{\\mathsf K}]+\\tau_T\\mathcal R(\\mathsf C)\n-\\lambda\\tau(ST^2)\\\\\n&amp;-\\tau[T^2(-a_R\\mathbf C-a_R\\lambda T-\\lambda I)].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFrom (8),\n\\[\n-\\mathbf C-\\lambda T=(\\mathcal H-\\mathbf C)/2\n\\ge(m_*+.341)I/2,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\Delta_p\\le\n\\nu[e_{\\mathsf K}]+\\tau_T\\mathcal R(\\mathsf C)\n-\\lambda\\tau(ST^2)-1.94\\tau T^2. \\tag{25}\n\\]</p><p>**Weighted Dirichlet term.**\nGaussian integration by parts and divided differences give\n\\[\nJ_I(u)=\\nu[\\overline{|u|^2}-|\\bar u|^2].\n\\]\nFor the weighted part, fix \\(\\ell,j\\), and expand in a spectral basis. The symmetrized scalar kernel of \\(J_T(u)\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac12\\bigl(\\overline{|u|^2}_{ij}\n+\\overline{|u|^2}_{kj}\\bigr)-\\bar u_{ij}\\cdot\\bar u_{kj}.\n\\]\nIt is unchanged by replacing \\(u\\) with \\(u-u_j\\).\nDefine\n\\[\nb_{ij}^*=\\overline{\\mathsf C}_{ij}-\\mathsf C_i\n-\\overline{|u-u_j|^2}_{ij}.\n\\]\nThe kernel of \\(\\tau_T\\mathcal R(\\mathsf C)-J_T(u)\\) becomes\n\\[\n\\tfrac12(b_{ij}^*+b_{kj}^*)\n+(\\bar u_{ij}-u_j)\\cdot(\\bar u_{kj}-u_j).\n\\]\nThe second part forms a positive Gram matrix, so its contraction with \\(T\\) is at most \\(t_+\\) times its trace. For the first part, interchange \\(i,k\\) and use Young’s inequality. Thus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tau_T\\mathcal R(\\mathsf C)-J_T(u)\n\\le{}&amp;\\lambda\\tau(ST^2)\n+\\frac1{4\\lambda}\\nu[(b_{ij}^*)^2]\\\\\n&amp;+t_+\\nu[|\\bar u_{ij}-u_j|^2]. \\qquad(26)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>**Commutator.**\nCommuting \\(M=M_\\ell\\) with (8) gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal H\\circ[M,T]=[M,\\mathbf K]+T\\circ[M,\\mathbf C].\n\\]\nThe Jordan map by \\(\\mathcal H\\ge m_*I\\) increases Hilbert–Schmidt norms by at least \\(m_*\\), also on skew-symmetric matrices. Splitting \\(T=t_cI+(T-t_cI)\\) and using\n\\(\\|A+B\\|_2^2\\le1.25\\|A\\|_2^2+5\\|B\\|_2^2\\), obtain\n\\[\nm_*^2\\|[M,T]\\|_2^2\n\\le1.25\\|[M,\\mathbf K+t_c\\mathbf C]\\|_2^2\n+5t_r^2\\|[M,\\mathbf C]\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nJensen in fixed coordinates, followed by passage to a spectral basis of \\(L\\), gives\n\\[\n.80\\|[L,T]\\|_2^2\n=.80\\sum_\\ell\\|[M_\\ell,T]\\|_2^2\n\\le\\nu[\\Gamma]. \\tag{27}\n\\]</p><p>**Assembly and strictness.**\nCombine (25)–(27). The \\(\\lambda\\tau(ST^2)\\) terms cancel:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta_p-J_\\Sigma(u)+.80\\|[L,T]\\|_2^2\n\\le{}&amp;\\nu\\left[\ne_{\\mathsf K}+\\Gamma+\\frac{(b_{ij}^*)^2}{4\\lambda}\n+t_+|\\bar u_{ij}-u_j|^2\n-\\bigl(\\overline{|u|^2}_{ij}-|\\bar u_{ij}|^2\\bigr)\\right]\\\\\n&amp;-1.94\\tau T^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSymmetrize using the symmetry of \\(\\nu\\).\nProposition 4 makes the symmetrized integrand strictly negative when \\(x_i\\ne x_j\\), and zero when they coincide. Polynomial growth ensures integrability. This proves the linear-field estimate.</p><p>Insert it into Proposition 9. The remaining contributions are the nonpositive \\(\\nu\\)-integral,\n\\(-.14\\tau T^2\\), and\n\\(-\\int(W-W_0)\\,d\\beta\\).\nSince \\(W-W_0&gt;0\\), equality \\(\\mathcal E=0\\) forces\n\\(T=0\\), diagonal support of \\(\\nu\\), and \\(\\beta=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Gaussian spectral identity — paper:", "statement": "<p>**Gaussian spectral identity — paper: Lemma 7.1.**</p><p>Let \\(F_*\\) be smooth with polynomially growing derivatives, and put \\(f=F_*''\\). Define\n\\[\nS=\\sum_\\ell M_\\ell^2,\\qquad\n\\bar f_{ij}=\\int_0^1f((1-t)x_i+tx_j)\\,dt,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal R(f)=\\sum_\\ell\\mathbb E\n \\left[M_\\ell\\circ(\\bar f\\odot M_\\ell)-M_\\ell^2\\circ f(L)\\right].\n\\]\nThe spectral expressions are returned to fixed coordinates before expectation. Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(NF_*)(L)]\n=(S-I)\\circ\\mathbb Ef(L)+\\mathcal R(f). \\tag{24}\n\\]\nThe construction is independent of the spectral basis, including repeated eigenvalues.</p>", "proof": "<p>The divided-difference derivative formula gives\n\\[\n(Dh(L)[M])_{ij}\n=\\left(\\int_0^1h'((1-t)x_i+tx_j)\\,dt\\right)M_{ij}.\n\\]\nIt follows first for polynomials and then by \\(C^1\\) approximation on compact spectral intervals, without differentiating eigenvectors.</p><p>Apply it to \\(h=F_*'\\):\n\\[\n\\partial_{G_\\ell}F_*'(L)=\\bar f\\odot M_\\ell.\n\\]\nPolynomial growth and \\(\\|L\\|_{\\rm op}\\le C|G|\\) justify Gaussian integration by parts, with cutoffs if necessary. Since \\(L\\) commutes with \\(F_*'(L)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(NF_*)(L)]\n=\\sum_\\ell M_\\ell\\circ\\mathbb E[\\bar f\\odot M_\\ell]-\\mathbb Ef(L).\n\\]\nAdd and subtract \\(S\\circ\\mathbb Ef(L)\\).\nBasis independence follows from the intrinsic divided-difference derivative; for the measure \\(\\nu\\), the squared block norm between two eigenspaces is basis-independent.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 12, "cite"], [6, 13, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [6, 20, "cite"], [6, 21, "cite"], [6, 24, "cite"], [6, 31, "cite"], [6, 36, "cite"], [7, 3, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 15, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 6, "cite"], [11, 6, "cite"], [12, 0, "cite"], [12, 24, "cite"], [12, 31, "cite"], [12, 36, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 36, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 22, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [15, 23, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"], [16, 20, "cite"], [16, 26, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [17, 20, "cite"], [17, 25, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [18, 26, "cite"], [19, 16, "cite"], [19, 27, "cite"], [20, 0, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 24, "cite"], [20, 31, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 24, "cite"], [21, 31, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [22, 37, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"], [24, 31, "cite"], [24, 36, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [25, 30, "cite"], [26, 30, "cite"], [27, 30, "cite"], [28, 31, "cite"], [28, 32, "cite"], [28, 34, "cite"], [29, 24, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [30, 24, "cite"], [31, 0, "cite"], [31, 36, "cite"], [32, 31, "cite"], [33, 34, "cite"], [33, 35, "cite"], [34, 31, "cite"], [35, 31, "cite"], [36, 0, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"]]});
